var e={"emergency-cardiac-arrest":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Cardiac arrest is sudden loss of effective cardiac output and circulation.`,`Diagnose it with two findings: **unresponsive** and **not breathing normally**.`,`Agonal gasps occur in up to 40% early on and mean arrest, not breathing.`,`Do not rely on a pulse check; it is wrong in about 4 of 10 attempts.`,`About **80%** of sudden cardiac death is due to coronary artery disease.`,`Survival falls **7-10% per minute** of VF without a shock.`,`The clinic owns the first three links: recognition, CPR and the AED.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify by the presenting rhythm, because it decides shock first or drug first.`,`**Shockable rhythms**: ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia.`,`**Non-shockable rhythms**: asystole and pulseless electrical activity (PEA).`,`PEA is an organised rhythm without a pulse, usually from a mechanical cause.`,`Peri-arrest states (slow, fast or hyperkalaemic rhythms with shock) are where arrest can still be prevented.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Most adult arrests come from an ischaemic or scarred myocardium causing VF.`,`Learn the reversible causes as the **4 Hs and 4 Ts**.`,`4 Hs: hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hypo- or hyperkalaemia and metabolic, hypothermia.`,`4 Ts: thrombosis (coronary or pulmonary), tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins.`,`In an Indian clinic think hypoxia, haemorrhage, dialysis hyperkalaemia, poisoning and MI.`,`Children, drowning and opioid overdose cause asphyxial arrest, so ventilation matters more.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. With no flow, consciousness is lost within **10-20 seconds**.`,`2. Irreversible brain injury begins after about **4-6 minutes** without flow.`,`3. Compressions give at best 25-30% of normal cardiac output.`,`4. Coronary perfusion pressure must exceed about **15-20 mmHg** for ROSC.`,`5. Every pause in compressions drops coronary perfusion pressure to zero within seconds.`,`6. A shock depolarises the whole myocardium so the sinus node can take over.`,`7. Adrenaline helps through alpha-1 vasoconstriction, which raises coronary perfusion pressure.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check the rhythm every **2 minutes**, keeping each pause under 10 seconds.`,`End-tidal CO2 under **10 mmHg** means poor compressions; change the compressor.`,`A sudden rise in end-tidal CO2 to 35-40 mmHg suggests ROSC.`,`Look for tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxidrome and a gravid uterus.`,`Check **capillary glucose** and core temperature in every arrest.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Start compressions within 10 seconds on a firm, flat surface.`,`Push **5-6 cm** deep at **100-120/min** and allow full recoil.`,`Give **30:2** compressions to breaths, each breath over 1 second.`,`Apply the AED as soon as it arrives and resume CPR right after the shock.`,`Swap the compressor every 2 minutes and keep compression fraction over 80%.`,`With an advanced airway, compress continuously and give one breath every 6 seconds.`,`Use IV first; use **intraosseous** access if IV fails twice.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Shockable rhythm: one shock of **150-200 J biphasic**, then CPR for 2 minutes.`,`After the **third shock**, give adrenaline 1 mg and amiodarone 300 mg.`,`Give amiodarone 150 mg after the fifth shock.`,`Non-shockable rhythm: give **adrenaline 1 mg** as soon as access is obtained.`,`Repeat adrenaline every 3-5 minutes throughout.`,`In refractory VF, change pad position to antero-posterior.`,`Consider stopping after 20 minutes of ALS with asystole and no reversible cause.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Children: give **5 rescue breaths** first, then 15:2 with two rescuers.`,`Children: shock at 4 J/kg and adrenaline 10 microgram/kg.`,`Infants: use the **two-thumb encircling** technique; the two-finger method is dropped.`,`Pregnancy over 20 weeks: manual left uterine displacement during CPR.`,`Pregnancy: plan **resuscitative hysterotomy within 5 minutes** if no ROSC.`,`Hypothermia, drowning and poisoning: continue CPR much longer.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Post-cardiac arrest syndrome has four parts: brain injury, myocardial stunning, reperfusion response, persisting cause.`,`Rib fractures during CPR are common and acceptable.`,`Over-bagging causes gastric inflation and lowers coronary perfusion.`,`Treat post-ROSC seizures with levetiracetam; seizure prophylaxis is not recommended.`,`Dialysis patient with peaked T waves: give calcium gluconate before arrest occurs.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Transfer every ROSC patient by ALS ambulance with a pre-alert to cath lab and ICU.`,`Post-ROSC targets: SpO2 **94-98%**, PaCO2 35-45 mmHg, MAP 65 mmHg or more.`,`Get a **12-lead ECG within 10 minutes** of ROSC; ST elevation means primary PCI.`,`Keep core temperature **37.7 C or below for 72 hours** in comatose patients.`,`Delay neuroprognostication to at least 72 hours, using several tests.`,`Screen first-degree relatives of young sudden cardiac death victims.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in cardiac arrest`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Adrenaline (1:10,000)`,`1 mg IV/IO; child 10 microgram/kg`,`Every 3-5 min`,`Arrhythmia; flush 20 mL saline`],[`Amiodarone`,`300 mg IV; child 5 mg/kg`,`After 3rd shock; 150 mg after 5th`,`Hypotension, bradycardia, phlebitis`],[`Lidocaine`,`1-1.5 mg/kg IV, then 50 mg`,`Only if no amiodarone`,`Seizures, confusion; max 3 mg/kg`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`2 g IV over 1-2 min`,`Single dose`,`Torsades or low magnesium only`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`30 mL IV`,`Single dose`,`Necrosis if extravasated`],[`Insulin with glucose`,`Insulin 10 units with 25 g glucose IV`,`Single dose`,`Hypoglycaemia; check glucose hourly 6 h`],[`Sodium bicarbonate 8.4%`,`50 mL (50 mmol) IV`,`Single dose`,`Only hyperkalaemia or tricyclic overdose`],[`Levetiracetam (post-ROSC)`,`40-60 mg/kg IV (max 4.5 g)`,`For seizures only`,`No prophylaxis`]]},{heading:`Rhythm decides the first action`,columns:[`Rhythm`,`ECG look`,`Shock?`,`Adrenaline timing`],rows:[[`VF`,`Chaotic waves, no QRS`,`Yes, at once`,`After 3rd shock`],[`Pulseless VT`,`Broad regular QRS over 150/min`,`Yes, at once`,`After 3rd shock`],[`Asystole`,`Flat line; check leads and gain`,`No`,`As soon as access`],[`PEA`,`Organised rhythm, no pulse`,`No`,`As soon as access`]]},{heading:`BLS by age`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Adult`,`Child`,`Infant`],rows:[[`Depth`,`5-6 cm`,`About 5 cm (one-third)`,`About 4 cm (one-third)`],[`Ratio`,`30:2`,`15:2 (two rescuers)`,`15:2 (two rescuers)`],[`Rescue breaths first`,`No`,`Yes, 5`,`Yes, 5`],[`Technique`,`Two hands, lower sternum`,`One or two hands`,`Two-thumb encircling`],[`Shock energy`,`150-200 J biphasic`,`4 J/kg`,`4 J/kg`]]}],remember:[`Unresponsive plus not breathing normally is arrest; skip the pulse hunt.`,`Push hard, push fast, full recoil, pauses under 10 seconds.`,`Arrest adrenaline 1 mg IV of 1:10,000; anaphylaxis 0.5 mg IM of 1:1000.`,`Shock first in VF; drug first in asystole and PEA.`,`No atropine in arrest; bicarbonate only for hyperkalaemia or tricyclics.`,`4 Hs and 4 Ts: hypoxia, hypovolaemia, potassium, hypothermia; thrombosis, tension, tamponade, toxins.`,`No one is dead until warm and dead.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Cardiac arrest (BLS and ACLS)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Sudden loss of effective cardiac output; bedside diagnosis by **unresponsive + absent or agonal breathing** - pulse check unreliable, max 10 s. Agonal gasps (up to 40%) = arrest.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By presenting rhythm: **shockable (VF, pulseless VT)** vs **non-shockable (asystole, PEA)** - decides shock first or adrenaline first. Peri-arrest states: brady under 40 with shock, broad-complex tachy with hypotension, hyperkalaemic ECG.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`About **80% coronary artery disease**; cardiomyopathy, channelopathy (family SCD under 40); clinic causes: **hypoxia, haemorrhage, dialysis hyperkalaemia, poisoning, MI**; **4 Hs and 4 Ts**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`No flow -> brain O2 gone in 10-20 s -> neuronal injury after **4-6 min**; compressions -> coronary perfusion pressure over 15-20 mmHg -> ROSC; every pause -> CPP falls to zero; survival falls **7-10% per minute** of VF without shock.`],[`Key history`,`Witnessed or not, time of collapse, bystander CPR; chest pain, palpitations, exertional syncope; dialysis, insulin/sulfonylurea, recent surgery or cancer (PE), poison, pregnancy, DNACPR.`],[`Key examination`,`Rhythm every **2 min** (pause under 10 s); **ETCO2 under 10 mmHg** = poor CPR, sudden rise to **35-40** = ROSC; tension (deviated trachea, absent breath sounds), tamponade (raised JVP), toxidrome, gravid uterus; glucose and temperature.`],[`Investigations`,`AED/monitor rhythm only to start; **capillary glucose**; blood gas with K+; POCUS during rhythm checks; **12-lead ECG within 10 min of ROSC**; post-ROSC troponin, electrolytes, CXR, CT head if cause unclear.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**30:2**, depth **5-6 cm**, rate **100-120/min**, full recoil, compression fraction over 80%; AED as soon as available - shock then **resume CPR at once**; swap compressor every 2 min; advanced airway -> continuous compressions + **1 breath every 6 s**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Adrenaline 1 mg IV/IO (1:10,000) every 3-5 min** - at once in asystole/PEA, **after 3rd shock** in VF/pVT; **amiodarone 300 mg after 3rd shock, 150 mg after 5th** (or lidocaine 1-1.5 mg/kg); shock **150-200 J biphasic**; no atropine in arrest.`],[`Treatment - reversible causes`,`Hyperkalaemia: **calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL** + insulin 10 U with 25 g glucose + bicarbonate 50 mmol; torsades: **MgSO4 2 g**; PE: alteplase 50 mg then CPR **60-90 min**; refractory VF: vector change (antero-posterior pads).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**AED pads** right infraclavicular + left mid-axillary (V6), or antero-posterior; charge during compressions; **IO** (proximal tibia 2 cm below tuberosity or humerus) if IV fails twice - **ET drug route obsolete**; supraglottic airway/tube by skilled hands with **waveform capnography**; thump only for witnessed monitored VF without a defibrillator.`],[`Special groups`,`**Child**: 5 rescue breaths, **15:2**, 4 J/kg, adrenaline **10 microgram/kg**, amiodarone 5 mg/kg; infant two-thumb encircling. **Pregnancy over 20 wk**: manual left uterine displacement, **hysterotomy within 5 min**. Hypothermia: not dead until warm.`],[`Treatment - post-ROSC`,`SpO2 **94-98%**, PaCO2 **35-45**, **MAP 65 or more / SBP over 100**, glucose under 180 mg/dL, **temperature 37.7 C or below for 72 h**, prognosticate **at 72 h or later** with multimodal tests; STEMI -> primary PCI.`],[`Complications`,`**Post-cardiac arrest syndrome** (4 parts): hypoxic-ischaemic brain injury, myocardial stunning, sepsis-like reperfusion vasodilatation, persisting cause; rib/sternal fractures (acceptable), aspiration, gastric inflation from over-bagging; post-ROSC seizures - **levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg** (no prophylaxis); hypotension - NE 0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Every ROSC transferred by ALS ambulance with pre-alert to cath lab + ICU; ETCO2 under 10 -> change compressor; stop after **20 min ALS** with asystole, no reversible cause, ETCO2 under 10 - team decision.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`ICD if VF without reversible trigger; cardiac rehab; screen anxiety, depression, cognition; **first-degree relatives** of young SCD screened (ECG, echo, genetics); hot debrief and timed documentation.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`AED checked daily, adrenaline and amiodarone in date, backboard, BVM, full O2; **mock drill every quarter**; teach compression-only CPR to families; call **108**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`ERC/AHA 2025: infants **two-thumb encircling (or heel of one hand)** - two-finger dropped; fever prevention **37.7 C or below for 72 h**; adrenaline timing unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Shock first in VF, drug first in asystole; arrest adrenaline 1 mg IV 1:10,000, anaphylaxis 0.5 mg IM 1:1000; atropine has no place in arrest; bicarbonate only for hyperkalaemia or TCA.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`ALS algorithm in one line`,caption:`Condensed exam version of the adult ALS loop. [ERC 2025, AHA 2025]`,steps:[{label:`Unresponsive, not breathing normally`,detail:`Call 108 + AED; start CPR 30:2 within 10 s`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Attach pads, assess rhythm`,detail:`Pause under 10 s; charge during compressions`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Shockable: VF / pVT`,detail:`1 shock 150-200 J biphasic, CPR 2 min; after 3rd shock adrenaline 1 mg + amiodarone 300 mg; 150 mg after 5th`},{label:`Non-shockable: asystole / PEA`,detail:`CPR 2 min; adrenaline 1 mg IV/IO as soon as access, then every 3-5 min`},{label:`Throughout: treat 4 Hs and 4 Ts`,detail:`Hypoxia, hypovolaemia, K+/metabolic, hypothermia; thrombosis, tension, tamponade, toxins`,tone:`decision`},{label:`ROSC: ABCDE post-arrest bundle`,detail:`SpO2 94-98%, MAP 65+, ECG in 10 min, temp 37.7 C or below for 72 h`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Shockable against non-shockable arrest`,caption:`What the rhythm changes. [ERC 2025]`,columns:[`Item`,`VF / pulseless VT`,`Asystole / PEA`],rows:[[`First action`,`Shock 150-200 J biphasic (child 4 J/kg)`,`CPR + adrenaline as soon as IV/IO`],[`Adrenaline`,`1 mg after 3rd shock, then every 3-5 min`,`1 mg immediately, then every 3-5 min`],[`Antiarrhythmic`,`Amiodarone 300 mg (3rd shock), 150 mg (5th)`,`None`],[`Likely cause`,`Ischaemic or scarred myocardium`,`Hypoxia, hypovolaemia, tamponade, tension, PE, K+`],[`Atropine`,`No`,`No`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Hyperkalaemic arrest ladder`,caption:`The dialysis patient who arrests in clinic. [ERC 2025]`,steps:[{label:`Calcium - stabilise membrane`,detail:`Calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL IV (or chloride 10% 10 mL)`},{label:`Shift K+ into cells`,detail:`Soluble insulin 10 U + glucose 25 g IV; monitor glucose hourly`},{label:`Sodium bicarbonate`,detail:`8.4% 50 mL (50 mmol) IV, separate line from calcium`},{label:`Remove K+`,detail:`Dialysis; continue CPR meanwhile`,tone:`good`}]}]},"emergency-anaphylaxis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Anaphylaxis is a serious, **rapid-onset systemic hypersensitivity** reaction that can kill.`,`Death comes through airway, breathing or circulation failure.`,`Skin signs are **absent in up to 20%**, including fatal reactions.`,`Adult hypotension means systolic BP under **90 mmHg** or a fall over 30%.`,`**Biphasic** reactions recur in about 5%, usually within 12 hours.`,`Refractory anaphylaxis means no response after **two IM adrenaline** doses.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Use the **WAO 2020 criteria**; either criterion makes the diagnosis.`,`Criterion 1: acute skin or mucosal signs plus respiratory, circulatory or severe gut features.`,`Criterion 2: acute hypotension, bronchospasm or laryngeal involvement after a likely allergen.`,`Criterion 2 needs **no skin signs** at all.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Indian triggers: injectable antibiotics, diclofenac, iron sucrose, anti-snake venom, contrast and vaccines.`,`Bee and wasp stings and foods like peanut, prawn, egg and milk are common.`,`Poorly controlled **asthma** is the strongest risk factor for fatal food anaphylaxis.`,`**Beta-blockers** and ACE inhibitors predict severe, refractory reactions.`,`Co-factors include exercise, alcohol, NSAIDs, infection and menstruation.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. First exposure makes allergen-specific **IgE**, which arms mast cells and basophils.`,`2. Re-exposure cross-links IgE and triggers degranulation within seconds (type I reaction).`,`3. Histamine and tryptase release at once; leukotrienes, prostaglandins and PAF follow.`,`4. Vasodilatation and leak can lose 35% of plasma volume within 10 minutes.`,`5. Mucosal oedema causes stridor; smooth muscle spasm causes wheeze and cramps.`,`6. Adrenaline reverses vasodilatation, bronchodilates and **stops further mast cell release**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`If any airway, breathing or circulation problem exists, **give adrenaline before more history**.`,`Ask what was given, eaten or stung, and how long ago.`,`Injected triggers act within 5-30 minutes; foods within 30 minutes to 2 hours.`,`Ask about previous reactions, asthma, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors and pregnancy.`,`Vasovagal attack shows bradycardia, pallor, no rash and recovery lying flat.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Airway: tongue or uvular swelling, **stridor** and hoarse voice.`,`Breathing: wheeze, silent chest and falling SpO2.`,`Circulation: tachycardia, low BP and slow capillary refill.`,`**Bradycardia** in a shocked patient is a late, pre-arrest sign.`,`Repeat ABCDE every 5 minutes to decide on another adrenaline dose.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`No test should delay adrenaline; diagnosis is clinical.`,`Take **serum tryptase** soon after treatment, again at 1-2 hours, and at 24 hours.`,`A normal tryptase does not exclude anaphylaxis, especially with food.`,`Allergy tests are done at **4-6 weeks**; earlier tests may be falsely negative.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Call for help and remove the trigger, but never delay adrenaline.`,`**Adrenaline 1:1000 IM** into the anterolateral mid-thigh, 0.01 mg/kg, maximum 0.5 mg.`,`Repeat IM adrenaline **every 5 minutes** if there is no improvement.`,`**Lie flat with legs raised**; never stand or walk the patient.`,`Give high-flow oxygen 15 L/min and a crystalloid bolus if shocked.`,`There is no absolute contraindication to adrenaline in anaphylaxis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`After two IM doses, start a monitored **low-dose IV adrenaline infusion**.`,`Never give 1:1000 adrenaline as an IV bolus.`,`Persistent wheeze: nebulised salbutamol 5 mg after adrenaline.`,`Beta-blocked and not responding: **glucagon 1-2 mg IV** over 5 minutes.`,`Antihistamines and steroids are not first-line; they do not treat shock or airway.`,`Call airway help early at the first sign of stridor or tongue swelling.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Observe **6 hours** after one dose of adrenaline.`,`Observe **12 hours** or admit after two doses or a severe reaction.`,`Discharge with **two auto-injectors** or a kit and a written action plan.`,`Refer every patient to an allergy clinic to confirm the trigger.`,`Label the allergy by name and report drug reactions to pharmacovigilance.`,`Prevention: ask about allergy before every injection and observe 30 minutes after.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in anaphylaxis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Adrenaline 1:1000`,`0.01 mg/kg IM thigh; max 0.5 mg`,`Repeat every 5 min`,`Tremor, palpitations; never IV bolus`],[`Adrenaline infusion`,`10 microgram/mL at 0.5-1 mL/kg/h`,`Titrate; refractory cases`,`Arrhythmia; continuous monitoring`],[`Crystalloid`,`Adult 500-1000 mL; child 10 mL/kg`,`Repeat to BP`,`Overload in cardiac or renal disease`],[`Salbutamol nebulised`,`5 mg (2.5 mg under 5 years)`,`Repeat as needed`,`Tremor, low potassium`],[`Glucagon`,`1-2 mg IV over 5 min`,`Repeatable, then infusion`,`Vomiting; for beta-blocked patients`],[`Chlorphenamine or cetirizine`,`Chlorphenamine 10 mg IM; cetirizine 10 mg oral`,`After stabilisation only`,`Sedation; skin symptoms only`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`200 mg IV (child 50-100 mg)`,`Once`,`Not routine; refractory or asthma`]]},{heading:`IM adrenaline dose by age`,columns:[`Age`,`Dose of 1:1000`,`Volume`],rows:[[`Adult and over 12 years`,`0.5 mg`,`0.5 mL`],[`6-12 years`,`0.3 mg`,`0.3 mL`],[`6 months to 6 years`,`0.15 mg`,`0.15 mL`],[`Under 6 months`,`0.1-0.15 mg`,`0.1-0.15 mL`]]},{heading:`Anaphylaxis and its mimics`,columns:[`Feature`,`Anaphylaxis`,`Vasovagal`,`Acute asthma`],rows:[[`Pulse`,`Fast (slow is pre-arrest)`,`Slow`,`Fast`],[`Skin`,`Urticaria, flushing (absent in 20%)`,`Pale, no rash`,`No rash`],[`BP`,`Low`,`Low, recovers lying flat`,`Normal`],[`Airway`,`Stridor, swelling possible`,`Normal`,`Wheeze only`]]}],remember:[`Adrenaline 1:1000 IM thigh, 0.01 mg/kg, max 0.5 mg; repeat every 5 minutes.`,`No rash does not mean no anaphylaxis.`,`Lie flat, legs up; standing can cause empty-ventricle arrest.`,`Beta-blocker plus no response to adrenaline means glucagon.`,`Observe 6 hours after one dose; 12 hours after two.`,`Two auto-injectors, a written plan and an allergy referral at discharge.`]},updated:[`GINA 2026: where features of both anaphylaxis and asthma are present, adrenaline is given first, then bronchodilators`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Anaphylaxis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Serious, **rapid-onset systemic hypersensitivity** reaction that can kill through **airway, breathing or circulation** failure; skin signs absent in **up to 20%**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IgE-mediated** (drugs, foods, venom) vs **non-IgE direct mast cell activation** (MRGPRX2: vancomycin, opioids, contrast, NMBs) - identical treatment. **Refractory** = A/B/C compromise despite **2 IM doses**; **biphasic** in about 5% (3-20%), usually within 12 h; Kounis = allergic coronary spasm.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**WAO 2020**: (1) acute skin/mucosal signs **plus** respiratory, circulatory (**adult SBP under 90 or over 30% fall**) or severe GI features; (2) **hypotension, bronchospasm or laryngeal involvement** after a likely allergen **even without skin signs**. Clinical diagnosis - adrenaline before any test.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`India: **injectable antibiotics, diclofenac, iron sucrose, ASV, contrast, vaccines, stings, foods**. Severity: uncontrolled asthma, **beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors**, co-factors (exercise, alcohol, NSAIDs).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`IgE on mast cells -> re-exposure cross-links -> **histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes, PAF** -> vasodilatation + leak (up to 35% volume in 10 min) + bronchospasm + mucosal oedema -> shock/arrest; empty-ventricle arrest on standing.`],[`Key history`,`What was given/eaten/stung and **when** (injection 5-30 min); previous reactions; beta-blocker/ACE inhibitor; asthma; pregnancy; isolated angioedema on ACE inhibitor = bradykinin (poor adrenaline response).`],[`Key examination`,`ABCDE: stridor, hoarse voice, tongue swelling; wheeze, SpO2; **SBP under 90 or over 30% fall**, tachycardia (bradycardia = pre-arrest); urticaria; repeat **every 5 min**.`],[`Investigations`,`None before adrenaline. **Mast cell tryptase**: ASAP, **1-2 h (max 4 h)**, baseline at 24 h; rise over 1.2 x baseline + 2 supports; normal does not exclude. Allergy tests at **4-6 weeks**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Adrenaline 1:1000 IM anterolateral thigh 0.01 mg/kg, max 0.5 mg**: adult/over 12 y **0.5 mg**, 6-12 y **0.3 mg**, 6 m-6 y **0.15 mg**; **repeat every 5 min**; lie flat, legs up; O2 15 L/min; crystalloid 500-1000 mL (child 10 mL/kg).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Refractory: **IV adrenaline infusion** 1 mg in 100 mL at 0.5-1 mL/kg/h; beta-blocked: **glucagon 1-2 mg IV**; bronchospasm: salbutamol 5 mg neb; stridor: neb adrenaline 5 mL 1:1000; antihistamine/steroid only after stabilisation.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Auto-injector**: blue cap off, orange tip to outer thigh, push till click, **hold 3 s**, call 108, 2nd device at 5 min. Kit: 0.5 mL of 1 mg/mL in 1 mL syringe IM. Intranasal adrenaline 2 mg (1 mg for 15-30 kg) where approved.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: same IM dose, left lateral. **Child**: age-band dose, 0.15 mg device 7.5-25 kg. **Elderly/cardiac**: no absolute contraindication. **Asthma + anaphylaxis**: adrenaline before bronchodilator.`],[`Complications`,`Airway obstruction, refractory shock, **Kounis syndrome**, biphasic reaction, arrest (PEA from empty ventricle); IV bolus 1:1000 error -> VT, MI, pulmonary oedema.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Stridor/tongue swelling, SBP under 90, no response after 2 doses, bradycardia, beta-blocked non-responder, anaphylaxis during iron/ASV/contrast.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Observe **6 h after one dose**, **12 h or admit** after 2 doses/severe/asthma; discharge with **2 auto-injectors or kit**, written plan, hands-on training, **allergy referral**, labelled allergy; report to PvPI/AEFI.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Ask allergy before every injection; adrenaline drawn up before ASV/iron/antibiotics; **30-min observation** after vaccines and injections; venom immunotherapy after sting anaphylaxis.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`GINA 2026: mixed asthma-anaphylaxis picture -> **adrenaline first, then bronchodilators**; RCUK 2021 anaphylaxis guideline remains current (RCUK/ERC 2025 cite it unchanged).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`No rash does not exclude anaphylaxis; never stand the patient up; steroids and antihistamines are not first-line and do not prevent biphasic reactions; beta-blocker + no response = glucagon.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Anaphylaxis: the first 10 minutes`,caption:`Condensed exam version. [RCUK 2021, ERC 2025]`,steps:[{label:`Sudden A, B or C problem after a trigger`,detail:`Usually skin signs - but absent in up to 20%`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Call for help, remove trigger, lie flat`,detail:`Legs raised; sit up only if breathing is the problem and BP normal`},{label:`IM adrenaline 1:1000 into thigh`,detail:`0.01 mg/kg, max 0.5 mg; 0.3 mg 6-12 y; 0.15 mg 6 m-6 y`},{label:`Improved after 5 min?`,detail:`Reassess ABCDE every 5 min`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No: repeat IM dose + fluids + O2`,detail:`Crystalloid 500-1000 mL (child 10 mL/kg)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Still no after 2 doses: refractory`,detail:`IV adrenaline infusion 0.5-1 mL/kg/h of 10 microgram/mL; glucagon if beta-blocked`},{label:`Yes: observe 6-12 h, discharge kit`,detail:`2 auto-injectors, plan, training, allergy referral`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Drug ladder in anaphylaxis`,caption:`Order of use - everything after adrenaline is an adjunct. [RCUK 2021]`,steps:[{label:`IM adrenaline (repeat every 5 min)`,detail:`Adult 0.5 mg of 1:1000 in anterolateral thigh`,tone:`good`},{label:`Oxygen and IV fluid`,detail:`15 L/min then SpO2 94-98%; 500-1000 mL crystalloid boluses`},{label:`IV adrenaline infusion (monitored)`,detail:`1 mg in 100 mL saline at 0.5-1 mL/kg/h; never 1:1000 IV bolus`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Glucagon if beta-blocked`,detail:`1-2 mg IV over 5 min, then 5-15 microgram/min`},{label:`Noradrenaline or vasopressin (ICU)`,detail:`For hypotension refractory to adrenaline and fluids`},{label:`After stabilisation only`,detail:`Cetirizine 10 mg / chlorphenamine 10 mg; hydrocortisone 200 mg if refractory or asthma`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Anaphylaxis against its look-alikes`,caption:`Quick bedside separation. [RCUK 2021]`,columns:[`Feature`,`Anaphylaxis`,`Vasovagal`,`Acute asthma`,`Bradykinin angioedema`],rows:[[`Pulse`,`Tachycardia`,`Bradycardia`,`Tachycardia`,`Normal`],[`Skin`,`Urticaria, flushing (absent in 20%)`,`Pale, clammy, no rash`,`Normal`,`Angioedema without urticaria`],[`BP`,`Low or falling`,`Low, recovers lying flat`,`Normal`,`Normal`],[`Clue`,`Trigger minutes before`,`Fear, needle, standing`,`Known asthma, no skin/BP change`,`ACE inhibitor or family history`],[`Treatment`,`IM adrenaline`,`Lie flat, legs up`,`Salbutamol, steroid`,`Stop ACEi; poor adrenaline response`]]}]},"emergency-shock":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Shock is acute circulatory failure where **oxygen delivery fails to meet cellular demand**.`,`It is a perfusion problem, not a blood pressure number.`,`**Hypotension is a late sign**; up to 30% of blood volume can be lost first.`,`Key numbers: MAP under **65 mmHg**, lactate over **2 mmol/L**, urine under 0.5 mL/kg/h.`,`Shock index (heart rate divided by systolic BP) over 0.9 suggests significant blood loss.`,`**Septic shock** needs a vasopressor for MAP 65 mmHg and lactate over 2 despite fluid.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Hypovolaemic**: haemorrhage, diarrhoea, burns and DKA reduce circulating volume.`,`**Distributive**: sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock, adrenal crisis.`,`**Cardiogenic**: MI, myocarditis, arrhythmia, valve failure, peripartum cardiomyopathy.`,`**Obstructive**: tension pneumothorax, tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism.`,`Stages run from compensated to decompensated to irreversible; treat early.`,`Shock is often mixed, so reclassify after every intervention.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Oxygen delivery depends on cardiac output and arterial oxygen content.`,`2. Each shock type breaks one link: preload, contractility, afterload or vessel tone.`,`3. Baroreceptors raise sympathetic outflow, causing tachycardia and cold, clammy skin.`,`4. Renin-angiotensin and ADH retain water, so urine output falls early.`,`5. Starved cells switch to anaerobic glycolysis and produce **lactate**.`,`6. Ion pumps fail, cells swell and organs fail if shock is not corrected.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about bleeding: trauma, haematemesis, melaena, heavy vaginal bleeding or missed period.`,`Ask about fluid loss: diarrhoea, vomiting, polyuria with high sugar, burns.`,`Ask about fever and its day; shock on **day 4-6** suggests dengue.`,`Ask about chest pain, breathlessness and recent delivery for cardiogenic shock.`,`Ask about long-term steroids, injections, stings, poisons and snake bite.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Capillary refill over **3 seconds** and knee mottling mean poor perfusion.`,`Check the three windows: skin, brain (confusion) and kidney (urine output).`,`**JVP plus lung fields** sorts the type of shock at the bedside.`,`Warm skin with bradycardia suggests **neurogenic shock**.`,`Look for tracheal deviation, muffled heart sounds and pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg.`,`Passive leg raise for 1 minute predicts fluid responsiveness.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`At cannulation, draw cross-match, Hb, glucose, creatinine, electrolytes and lactate.`,`Take **two sets of blood cultures** before antibiotics.`,`Repeat lactate within **2-4 hours**; a falling value means resuscitation is working.`,`Do a **urine pregnancy test** in every woman of reproductive age with shock.`,`Get an ECG within 10 minutes and bedside ultrasound to find the type.`,`Never delay treatment for CVP or CT in an unstable patient.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Lie flat, give oxygen to SpO2 94-98% and stop visible bleeding.`,`Insert two large-bore cannulae; use intraosseous access if two attempts fail.`,`Give fluid as boluses over 15-30 minutes and **reassess after each**.`,`**Stop fluid** when JVP rises, crackles appear or SpO2 falls.`,`Septic shock: at least **30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid** within 3 hours.`,`Septic shock: **antibiotics within 1 hour**, after blood cultures.`,`Catheterise and target urine at least 0.5 mL/kg/h.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Start **noradrenaline** early if MAP stays under 65 mmHg after 1-2 L.`,`Start noradrenaline peripherally in a large proximal vein; do not wait for a central line.`,`Add vasopressin 0.03 units/min when noradrenaline reaches 0.25-0.5 microgram/kg/min.`,`Add dobutamine for low cardiac output with adequate filling and MAP.`,`Hydrocortisone 50 mg 6-hourly is suggested in septic shock still needing vasopressor.`,`Dopamine is not first-line, and renal-dose dopamine protects nothing.`,`Control the source (drain, remove line, surgery) within 6-12 hours.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Haemorrhage: limit crystalloid to about 1 L, then blood in **1:1:1** ratio.`,`Haemorrhage: permissive hypotension (systolic 80-90 mmHg) until bleeding stops, except head injury.`,`Children: 10-20 mL/kg boluses; without ICU, give boluses only for hypotension (FEAST).`,`Cardiogenic shock from MI needs primary PCI; avoid nitrates in RV infarction.`,`Adrenal crisis: **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV** at once plus saline.`,`Severe anaemia with heart failure: transfuse slowly with furosemide cover.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Reassess every 15 minutes in the first hour.`,`Shock persisting after 30 mL/kg or needing noradrenaline means **ICU transfer**.`,`Transfer with escort, oxygen, running line and written notes.`,`Dengue: check haematocrit 4-6 hourly in the critical phase.`,`Steroid-dependent patients leave with sick-day rules and an emergency hydrocortisone kit.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in shock`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Balanced crystalloid`,`Septic: 30 mL/kg IV in 500 mL boluses`,`Within 3 h, reassess each`,`Pulmonary and tissue oedema`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV infusion`,`Titrate to MAP 65`,`Extravasation necrosis, gut ischaemia`],[`Vasopressin`,`0.03 units/min fixed IV`,`Added to noradrenaline`,`Digital ischaemia, hyponatraemia`],[`Adrenaline infusion`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV`,`Refractory shock`,`Arrhythmia, raised lactate`],[`Dobutamine`,`2.5-20 microgram/kg/min IV`,`Low output states`,`Tachycardia, can drop BP`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`50 mg IV 6-hourly; crisis 100 mg stat`,`While vasopressor needed`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection risk`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g IV over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h`,`Only within 3 h of injury`,`Rare thrombosis, seizures`],[`Ceftriaxone (sepsis)`,`2 g IV`,`Within 1 h; review 48-72 h`,`Broaden if hospital-acquired`]]},{heading:`Bedside types of shock`,columns:[`Type`,`JVP and lungs`,`Skin and pulse`,`First action`],rows:[[`Hypovolaemic`,`Flat JVP, clear lungs`,`Cold, narrow pulse pressure`,`Stop bleeding, fluid, blood`],[`Septic`,`Low JVP, clear or focal crackles`,`Warm early, wide pulse pressure`,`Cultures, antibiotics, fluid, noradrenaline`],[`Neurogenic`,`Flat JVP`,`Warm, bradycardia`,`Fluid, noradrenaline, atropine`],[`Cardiogenic`,`Raised JVP, crackles`,`Cold, mottled`,`Reperfusion, noradrenaline, dobutamine`],[`Obstructive`,`Raised JVP, clear lungs`,`Cold, pulsus paradoxus`,`Decompress, drain, thrombolyse`]]},{heading:`Targets in the first hours`,columns:[`Measure`,`Target`],rows:[[`MAP`,`65 mmHg or more (60-65 if aged 65 or over)`],[`Capillary refill`,`Under 3 seconds`],[`Urine output`,`0.5 mL/kg/h adult; 1 mL/kg/h child`],[`Lactate`,`Falling on repeat at 2-4 hours`],[`SpO2`,`94-98%`]]}],remember:[`Hypotension is late; diagnose shock by refill, sensorium, urine and lactate.`,`JVP plus lungs: flat is fluid, full with crackles is pump, full and clear is obstruction.`,`Sepsis: cultures, antibiotics in 1 hour, 30 mL/kg, noradrenaline to MAP 65.`,`Fluid is a drug: bolus, reassess, stop when JVP rises or crackles appear.`,`Neurogenic shock is the only shock with warm skin and bradycardia.`,`Bleeding needs haemostasis, blood 1:1:1 and TXA within 3 hours.`]},updated:[`SSC 2026: NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS preferred over qSOFA as a single sepsis screening tool`,`SSC 2026: at least 30 mL/kg crystalloid in first 3 h; balanced crystalloids suggested; crystalloid alone suggested over adding albumin`,`SSC 2026: antibiotics immediately (ideally within 1 h) for shock or probable sepsis; within 3 h after rapid assessment for possible sepsis without shock`,`SSC 2026: initial MAP 65 mmHg, 60-65 mmHg suggested for adults aged 65 or over; vasopressors started peripherally rather than delayed`,`SSC 2026: capillary refill time suggested as an adjunct to guide resuscitation; fluids not continued until lactate normalises; fluid removal after resuscitation`,`SSC 2026: IV corticosteroids suggested in septic shock (hydrocortisone 200 mg/day usual); fludrocortisone optional`,`SSC Children 2026: 10-20 mL/kg balanced crystalloid boluses, up to 40-60 mL/kg in first hour with ICU, up to 40 mL/kg only if hypotensive without ICU`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Shock and the first hour`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute circulatory failure with **inadequate tissue O2 delivery** -> cellular dysoxia; a perfusion problem, not a BP number. Cut-offs: **MAP under 65**, SBP under 90, **shock index over 0.9-1.0**, **lactate over 2 mmol/L**, urine under 0.5 mL/kg/h.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Hypovolaemic, distributive** (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic, adrenal), **cardiogenic, obstructive** (tension, tamponade, PE). Stages: **compensated** (normal BP), decompensated, irreversible. Often **mixed** - reclassify after every intervention. Bedside sort: **JVP + lung fields**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Haemorrhage (trauma, GI, ectopic, PPH), diarrhoea, DKA, burns; sepsis (pneumonia, UTI, abdomen, malaria, **dengue day 4-6**); MI, peripartum cardiomyopathy; tension, tamponade, PE; steroid withdrawal; age over 65, CKD, CLD, HIV.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Low DO2 -> baroreflex sympathetic surge (tachycardia, cold skin, narrow pulse pressure) -> RAAS/ADH oliguria -> anaerobic glycolysis -> **lactate** -> Na-K pump failure -> organ failure; sepsis: NO-driven vasodilatation + capillary leak.`],[`Key history`,`Bleeding (haematemesis, melaena, **missed period**), fluid loss, fever and day of illness, chest pain, allergen exposure, **long-term steroids**, poisons/snakebite, trauma, malignancy/immobility.`],[`Key examination`,`Three windows: **skin (CRT over 3 s, mottling), brain (confusion), kidney (oliguria)**. **JVP + lungs**: flat + clear = hypovolaemic/distributive; raised + crackles = cardiogenic; raised + clear = obstructive or RV infarct. Warm + bradycardia = neurogenic. Passive leg raise for fluid responsiveness.`],[`Investigations`,`At cannulation: group and cross-match, Hb, glucose, creatinine, electrolytes, **lactate (repeat at 2-4 h)**, **2 sets blood cultures** before antibiotics; **urine pregnancy test**; ECG in 10 min; gas with base deficit; POCUS/RUSH, eFAST; dengue NS1, malaria RDT.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Sepsis-3**: infection + organ dysfunction (SOFA rise 2+); **septic shock** = vasopressor needed for **MAP 65** + **lactate over 2** despite adequate fluid (mortality over 40%). **Screen with NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS rather than qSOFA** as a single tool (SSC 2026) - qSOFA too insensitive to rule out.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Lie flat, O2 to 94-98%, **2 large-bore cannulae** (IO if 2 fails in 90 s), stop bleeding (pressure, tourniquet, **pelvic binder**), fluid in **500 mL boluses with reassessment** and a stop rule (JVP up, crackles, SpO2 falls), catheter.`],[`Treatment - septic shock bundle`,`**Antibiotics immediately, ideally within 1 h** (ceftriaxone 2 g; pip-taz 4.5 g or meropenem 1 g if hospital-acquired); **at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid in 3 h**, crystalloid alone (no routine albumin); source control in 6-12 h; possible sepsis without shock -> antibiotics within 3 h if concern persists.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Noradrenaline 0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min**, start **peripherally**, target **MAP 65 (60-65 if 65 or over)**; add **vasopressin 0.03 U/min** at NE 0.25-0.5; adrenaline next; **dobutamine 2.5-20** for low output; **hydrocortisone 50 mg 6-hourly** suggested in septic shock; dopamine not first-line.`],[`Treatment - type-specific`,`Haemorrhage: about 1 L crystalloid then **blood 1:1:1**, **TXA 1 g within 3 h**, permissive SBP 80-90 (not head injury). Anaphylaxis: **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM**. Cardiogenic: PCI, NE + dobutamine, 250 mL only if lungs clear. Obstructive: decompress, pericardiocentesis, alteplase 100 mg/2 h for PE. Adrenal: **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Child**: 10-20 mL/kg balanced boluses, up to **40-60 mL/kg in 1 h with ICU**; without ICU only if hypotensive (max 40 mL/kg) - FEAST. **Dengue**: compensated 5-10 mL/kg/h adult (10-20 child) x 1 h, hypotensive 20 mL/kg over 15-30 min, taper by haematocrit. **Severe anaemia**: slow packed cells + furosemide. **Elderly**: MAP 60-65.`],[`Complications`,`AKI, ARDS, DIC, lethal triad (hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy + low Ca2+), fluid overload, mesenteric/digital ischaemia from vasopressors, multi-organ failure, post-sepsis syndrome.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`CRT over 3 s with mottled knees in fever; dengue pulse pressure 20 or less; shock in woman of reproductive age (ectopic); raised JVP + clear lungs; **lactate 4 or more or not falling**; shock after 30 mL/kg -> NE and ICU.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Every 15 min in first hour: HR, BP, MAP, **CRT**, sensorium, urine 0.5 mL/kg/h; repeat lactate (do not chase normal lactate with fluid); **de-resuscitate once shock resolves**; post-sepsis review, vaccines; steroid sick-day rules.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SSC 2026**: NEWS2/SIRS over qSOFA; at least 30 mL/kg; crystalloid alone over albumin; MAP 60-65 if 65 or over; peripheral vasopressor start; CRT-guided resuscitation; steroids suggested; fluid removal after resuscitation; **SSC Children 2026** fluid caps.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Hypotension is late; shock index over 0.9 predicts blood loss; neurogenic is the only warm shock with bradycardia; renal-dose dopamine does nothing; after 1 L in haemorrhage the next fluid is blood.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Septic shock: first hour (SSC 2026)`,caption:`Condensed exam version of the updated bundle. [SSC 2026]`,steps:[{label:`Screen: NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS (not qSOFA alone)`,detail:`Fever + CRT over 3 s, confusion, oliguria, lactate over 2`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Shock or probable sepsis?`,detail:`Possible sepsis without shock: rapid assessment, antibiotics within 3 h if concern persists`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Cultures, then antibiotics within 1 h`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV; pip-taz 4.5 g or meropenem 1 g if hospital-acquired`},{label:`At least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid in 3 h`,detail:`500 mL boluses, reassess CRT, JVP, crackles, SpO2; no routine albumin`},{label:`MAP still under 65?`,detail:`Target 60-65 if aged 65 or over`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Noradrenaline, started peripherally`,detail:`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min; vasopressin 0.03 U/min at NE 0.25-0.5`},{label:`Persisting vasopressor need`,detail:`Hydrocortisone 50 mg IV 6-hourly (200 mg/day); source control 6-12 h`},{label:`Shock resolved: de-resuscitate`,detail:`Stop fluid, remove excess with diuretics; ICU transfer`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Four shock types at the bedside`,caption:`JVP plus lung fields sort the type. [Harrison 22e, SSC 2026]`,columns:[`Type`,`JVP / lungs`,`Skin and pulse`,`First move`],rows:[[`Hypovolaemic`,`Flat / clear`,`Cold, narrow pulse pressure`,`Stop bleeding, 1 L then blood 1:1:1, TXA`],[`Septic (distributive)`,`Low-normal / clear or focal`,`Warm early, wide pulse pressure, CRT over 3 s`,`Antibiotics 1 h, 30+ mL/kg, peripheral NE`],[`Anaphylactic / neurogenic`,`Flat / wheeze in anaphylaxis`,`Urticaria; or warm + bradycardia`,`IM adrenaline; or fluid, NE, atropine`],[`Cardiogenic`,`Raised / crackles`,`Cold, mottled`,`PCI, NE + dobutamine, cautious 250 mL`],[`Obstructive`,`Raised / clear`,`Pulsus paradoxus or absent breath sounds`,`Decompress, pericardiocentesis, thrombolysis`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Vasoactive ladder in septic shock`,caption:`Escalate, do not repeat failed fluid. [SSC 2026]`,steps:[{label:`Fluid: at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid`,detail:`In 3 h, boluses with reassessment and stop rule`},{label:`Noradrenaline (peripheral start)`,detail:`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min to MAP 65 (60-65 if 65+)`},{label:`Add vasopressin`,detail:`0.03 units/min fixed when NE 0.25-0.5 microgram/kg/min`},{label:`Add adrenaline`,detail:`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min if MAP still inadequate`},{label:`Hydrocortisone`,detail:`50 mg IV 6-hourly; fludrocortisone optional`},{label:`Dobutamine for low output`,detail:`2.5-20 microgram/kg/min with adequate MAP and filling`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"emergency-poisoning":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Most adult poisoning in India is **deliberate self-poisoning with pesticides**.`,`Most childhood poisoning is accidental: kerosene, medicines and household chemicals.`,`Common poisons: organophosphates, aluminium phosphide, rodenticides, yellow oleander, corrosives, paracetamol.`,`A toxic paracetamol dose is **150 mg/kg or 7.5 g** or more in 24 hours.`,`Every poisoning in India is a **medico-legal case**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify by **toxidrome**, because the bedside picture guides the first hour.`,`Cholinergic: pinpoint pupils, wet skin, bronchorrhoea, fasciculations.`,`Anticholinergic: dilated pupils, hot dry flushed skin, delirium, urinary retention.`,`Opioid: pinpoint pupils, coma and respiratory rate under 12.`,`Cardiac glycoside: bradycardia, heart block, vomiting and hyperkalaemia.`,`Sympathomimetic: dilated pupils, sweating, tachycardia and hyperthermia.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Organophosphates block **acetylcholinesterase**, so acetylcholine builds up at all synapses.`,`2. Muscarinic excess causes bronchorrhoea, which drowns the patient.`,`3. Nicotinic excess causes fasciculations, then weakness and paralysis.`,`4. The blocked enzyme 'ages' within hours to days; pralidoxime then fails.`,`5. Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors only, so it does not reverse weakness.`,`6. In paracetamol overdose, excess **NAPQI** depletes glutathione and kills zone 3 liver cells.`,`7. Aluminium phosphide releases phosphine, which blocks cytochrome oxidase and causes refractory shock.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Protect yourself first: gloves, remove clothing, wash the patient's skin.`,`Ask for the **container**; the label gives compound and concentration.`,`Ask how much and when; timing decides charcoal and paracetamol level timing.`,`Ask what a local practitioner already gave, such as atropine or lavage.`,`Take a psychiatric history once stable to judge suicide risk.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Always check capillary glucose: 'don't ever forget glucose'.`,`In organophosphate poisoning, **the chest, not the pupils**, is the atropine end-point.`,`Feel the axilla: wet in cholinergic, bone dry in anticholinergic poisoning.`,`Test neck flexion strength daily for intermediate syndrome.`,`Normal lips do not exclude oesophageal burns after corrosives.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Take an **ECG** in every deliberate overdose.`,`Potassium over **5.5 mmol/L** in oleander poisoning predicts death without Fab.`,`Check the paracetamol level at **4 hours or later**, never earlier.`,`Cholinesterase confirms organophosphate exposure but never delays atropine.`,`Check INR at 24 and 48 hours after superwarfarin ingestion.`,`Do not order a blind toxicology screen routinely.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Resuscitate first: airway, oxygen to SpO2 94-98%, IV access, fluids.`,`**Activated charcoal 1 g/kg** within 1 hour, only with a protected airway.`,`Gastric lavage is not routine; ipecac is abandoned.`,`Corrosives: **no emesis, no lavage, no charcoal, no neutralisation**.`,`Kerosene: no emesis or lavage; observe at least 6 hours.`,`Organophosphate: **atropine 1.8-3 mg IV**, doubled every 5 minutes until atropinised.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Atropinisation: clear chest, heart rate over 80, systolic over 80, dry axillae, pupils not pinpoint.`,`Then infuse **10-20% of the loading dose** per hour.`,`Atropine toxicity: stop, wait 30-60 minutes, restart at 70-80% of the rate.`,`Give pralidoxime only after atropine, as an infusion, never pushed fast.`,`Paracetamol: start **N-acetylcysteine** if the level is above the line or timing is unknown.`,`Aluminium phosphide has no antidote; give fluids, noradrenaline and early ICU care.`,`Yellow oleander: multiple-dose charcoal, atropine, then Fab or pacing; avoid IV calcium.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Inability to lift the head at 24-96 hours means **intermediate syndrome**; ventilate.`,`Stridor, drooling or hoarseness after a corrosive means early intubation.`,`Yellow phosphorus causes liver failure at 3-5 days after a well interval.`,`Avoid succinylcholine, aminoglycosides and morphine in organophosphate poisoning.`,`Corrosive survivors develop strictures at 3-6 weeks.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer all organophosphate, phosphide, oleander, paraquat and corrosive poisonings.`,`Transfer with a written list of every drug, dose and clock time.`,`Every self-harm patient needs a **psychosocial assessment** before discharge.`,`Review organophosphate survivors at 2-3 weeks for delayed neuropathy.`,`For unfamiliar compounds, call the AIIMS poison helpline **1800-116-117**.`,`Prevention: locked pesticide storage and never decanting kerosene into drink bottles.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antidotes and key drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Atropine`,`1.8-3 mg IV; child 0.02-0.05 mg/kg`,`Double every 5 min, then infusion`,`Delirium, hyperthermia, ileus, retention`],[`Pralidoxime`,`30 mg/kg IV over 15-30 min, then 8 mg/kg/h`,`Until atropine not needed 12-24 h`,`Laryngospasm, weakness if pushed fast`],[`N-acetylcysteine IV`,`150 mg/kg over 1 h, 50 over 4 h, 100 over 16 h`,`21 h; longer if ALT rising`,`Flushing, wheeze: slow, do not stop`],[`Naloxone`,`0.4 mg IV, repeat to 2-10 mg`,`Infusion for long-acting opioids`,`Acute withdrawal, agitation`],[`Vitamin K1`,`10 mg slow IV, then 10-50 mg oral daily`,`Weeks to months, by INR`,`Reaction if given fast IV`],[`Activated charcoal`,`1 g/kg (adult 50 g) oral`,`Within 1 h; repeat 4-6 h for oleander`,`Aspiration; not for corrosives or kerosene`],[`Digoxin-specific Fab`,`5-10 vials IV empirically`,`Repeat if needed`,`Hypokalaemia; pacing if unavailable`],[`Fomepizole (methanol)`,`15 mg/kg IV, then 10 mg/kg 12-hourly`,`With dialysis`,`Headache, nausea`]]},{heading:`Toxidromes at a glance`,columns:[`Toxidrome`,`Pupils`,`Skin`,`Key sign`,`First move`],rows:[[`Cholinergic`,`Pinpoint`,`Wet`,`Bronchorrhoea, fasciculations`,`Atropine, then pralidoxime`],[`Anticholinergic`,`Dilated`,`Hot and dry`,`Delirium, urinary retention`,`Cooling, benzodiazepine`],[`Opioid`,`Pinpoint`,`Normal or cool`,`Breathing under 12/min`,`Ventilate, naloxone`],[`Cardiac glycoside`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Heart block, high potassium`,`Charcoal, atropine, Fab`],[`Sympathomimetic`,`Dilated`,`Wet`,`Tachycardia, hyperthermia`,`Benzodiazepine, cooling`]]},{heading:`When to start NAC`,columns:[`Situation`,`Action`],rows:[[`Level at 4-8 h above line (150 mg/L at 4 h)`,`Start NAC`],[`Over 8 h after 150 mg/kg or more`,`Start NAC now, then check level`],[`Staggered or unknown time`,`Treat with NAC`],[`ALT raised or INR over 1.3`,`Continue NAC beyond 21 h`],[`pH under 7.30 after resuscitation`,`Urgent liver unit transfer`]]}],remember:[`Treat the patient, not the poison: ABCDE and glucose first.`,`DUMBELS for muscarinic excess: diarrhoea, urination, miosis, bronchorrhoea, emesis, lacrimation, salivation.`,`Atropine to a dry chest, doubling every 5 minutes; no maximum dose.`,`Anticholinergic: hot as a hare, blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, mad as a hatter.`,`NAC reaction means slow it, not stop it.`,`Corrosives: no emesis, no lavage, no charcoal, no neutralisation.`,`Preserve the container and inform police, but never delay treatment.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Poisoning in Indian practice`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Injury from a substance by ingestion, inhalation, injection or skin contact; adults - **deliberate pesticide self-poisoning**, children - **accidental kerosene, medicines, household chemicals**. Every case is **medico-legal**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By **toxidrome**: cholinergic, anticholinergic, opioid, sympathomimetic, sedative-hypnotic, cardiac glycoside. OP severity clinical (secretions, fasciculations, GCS, respiration); toxic paracetamol **150 mg/kg or 7.5 g** (UK assesses from 75 mg/kg; risk factors no longer change the line - MHRA 2012).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`OP/carbamates, **aluminium phosphide (Celphos)**, yellow phosphorus (Ratol), superwarfarins, **yellow oleander**, paraquat, corrosives, kerosene, paracetamol, benzodiazepines, methanol. Risk: farming households, depression, alcohol, unlocked storage, **toddlers 1-5 y**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`OP: AChE phosphorylation -> ACh excess -> muscarinic (wet, miosis, bradycardia) + nicotinic (fasciculation -> paralysis) -> enzyme **ages** -> oxime useless. Paracetamol: glutathione depleted -> **NAPQI** -> zone 3 necrosis. Phosphide -> phosphine -> cytochrome oxidase block -> refractory shock.`],[`Key history`,`**What** (container), **how much** (mouthful 20-30 mL), **when** (charcoal 1 h, level at 4 h, NAC 8 h), why, co-ingestants, what was already given; malnutrition, alcohol, enzyme inducers; psychiatric history once stable.`],[`Key examination`,`Pupils, axillae wet or dry, **chest crackles (atropine end-point)**, RR, pulse/ECG (block in oleander, broad QRS in TCA), smell (garlic, rotten fish, kerosene), neck flexion power, oral burns (normal lips do not exclude oesophageal burn).`],[`Investigations`,`**Capillary glucose**, **ECG**, K+ (over 5.5 in oleander = Fab/pacing), **paracetamol level at 4 h or later** (line 150 mg/L at 4 h; **UK single 100 mg/L line**), ALT/INR, gas with anion/osmolar gap; INR at 24-48 h for superwarfarin; no blind tox screen.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`ABCDE + glucose; decontaminate skin (soap, water); **activated charcoal 1 g/kg (50 g) within 1 h** with protected airway; **no lavage routinely**, never for corrosives/kerosene; no emesis.`],[`Treatment - organophosphate`,`**Atropine 1.8-3 mg IV, double every 5 min** to clear chest, HR over 80, SBP over 80, dry axillae, pupils not pinpoint; infusion **10-20% of loading dose/h**; **pralidoxime 30 mg/kg then 8 mg/kg/h** after atropine; avoid succinylcholine.`],[`Treatment - other antidotes`,`**NAC 150 mg/kg/1 h, 50 mg/kg/4 h, 100 mg/kg/16 h** (or 2-bag / SNAP); naloxone 0.4 mg titrated to RR over 12; **vitamin K1 10 mg IV** + FFP/PCC for superwarfarin; oleander - MDAC, atropine, **Fab or pacing**, no calcium; methanol - fomepizole 15 mg/kg; TCA - bicarbonate 1-2 mmol/kg.`],[`Special groups`,`**Corrosive**: NBM, PPI, early airway, endoscopy 12-48 h (Zargar). **Kerosene**: observe 6 h, CXR. **Phosphide**: no antidote - fluids, NE, bicarbonate, MgSO4, ICU. **Paraquat**: O2 only if very low SpO2. **Child**: atropine 0.02-0.05 mg/kg (min 0.1 mg). **Pregnancy**: same antidotes incl. NAC.`],[`Complications`,`OP **intermediate syndrome (24-96 h)** - neck flexor weakness, ventilate; OPIDN at 2-3 weeks; paracetamol liver failure (King's: pH under 7.30, or INR over 6.5 + creatinine over 3.4 + grade III-IV HE); corrosive stricture 3-6 weeks; Ratol liver failure day 3-5.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Wet chest + pinpoint pupils; cannot lift head; staggered/unknown-time paracetamol; stridor after corrosive; refractory shock with garlic smell; heart block + K+ over 5.5; bleeding days after rodenticide; well-looking Ratol.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Hourly atropine end-points, neck flexion, single breath count; transfer with **timed drug list**; **psychosocial assessment** before discharge for every self-harm; corrosive stricture surveillance; OPIDN review.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Locked pesticide storage, child-resistant packaging, no decanting into drink bottles, banning highly hazardous pesticides; **NPIC AIIMS 1800-116-117**; police intimation, preserve samples.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - Eddleston atropine regimen, NICE CKS paracetamol and AIIMS NPIC protocols remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Atropine to a dry chest, not to pupils or HR 140; oxime after atropine and useless once aged; NAC reaction means slow, not stop; corrosives - no emesis, lavage, charcoal or neutralisation.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Organophosphate poisoning: atropinise fast`,caption:`Condensed exam version. [Eddleston 2008]`,steps:[{label:`Wet chest, pinpoint pupils, sweating`,detail:`Decontaminate with gloves; ABC, O2, suction`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Atropine 1.8-3 mg IV bolus`,detail:`3-5 ampoules of 0.6 mg`},{label:`Atropinised at 5 min?`,detail:`Clear chest, HR over 80, SBP over 80, dry axillae, pupils not pinpoint`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No: double the dose`,detail:`3, 6, 12, 24 mg every 5 min - no maximum`},{label:`Yes: infusion 10-20% of loading dose/h`,detail:`Plus pralidoxime 30 mg/kg then 8 mg/kg/h`},{label:`Watch neck flexion and breath count`,detail:`Intermediate syndrome 24-96 h needs ventilation`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Paracetamol overdose to NAC`,caption:`Decision points by time since ingestion. [NICE CKS 2024]`,steps:[{label:`Toxic dose: 150 mg/kg or 7.5 g or more?`,detail:`UK assesses from 75 mg/kg; single 100 mg/L line, no high-risk line`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Under 8 h, single known time`,detail:`Level at 4 h or later; NAC if on/above line (150 mg/L; UK 100 mg/L at 4 h)`},{label:`Over 8 h, staggered or unknown time`,detail:`Start NAC at once, then level, ALT, INR`,tone:`warn`},{label:`NAC 21-hour regimen`,detail:`150 mg/kg/1 h, 50 mg/kg/4 h, 100 mg/kg/16 h; reaction - slow, chlorphenamine`},{label:`ALT or INR rising at end?`,detail:`Continue NAC; King's criteria -> liver unit`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Normal bloods: psychosocial assessment`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Three Indian killers without a simple antidote`,caption:`Supportive care decides survival. [AIIMS NPIC 2022]`,columns:[`Feature`,`Aluminium phosphide`,`Yellow oleander`,`Yellow phosphorus (Ratol)`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Phosphine blocks cytochrome oxidase`,`Cardiac glycoside blocks Na-K ATPase`,`Protoplasmic hepatotoxin`],[`Clue`,`Refractory shock, acidosis, garlic/fish smell`,`Vomiting, bradycardia, AV block, high K+`,`Well at first, jaundice day 3-5`],[`Key treatment`,`Fluids, NE, bicarbonate, MgSO4, ICU`,`MDAC, atropine, Fab or pacing`,`Supportive, liver unit referral`],[`Avoid`,`Delay in transfer; unventilated room`,`IV calcium`,`Discharging the well patient`]]}]},"emergency-burns":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A burn is tissue injury from thermal, electrical, chemical, radiation or friction energy.`,`In India, most are **flame burns in women** and **scalds in children**.`,`Extent is **% TBSA** of partial- and full-thickness burn only.`,`Simple erythema is **never counted** in TBSA.`,`Major burn: over **15% TBSA in adults** or over 10% in children, or inhalation injury.`,`Revised Baux score is age plus %TBSA plus 17 for inhalation injury.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify by **depth**, because depth decides healing and grafting.`,`Superficial (epidermal): red, dry, painful, heals in 5-7 days.`,`Superficial partial thickness: pink, moist, blistered, heals in 10-14 days.`,`Deep partial thickness: blotchy, reduced sensation, takes over 3 weeks.`,`Full thickness: white or leathery, **insensate**, heals only from the edges.`,`Sensation is more reliable than colour for judging depth.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Jackson's zones: central coagulation, surrounding stasis, outer hyperaemia.`,`2. The **zone of stasis** is salvageable for 24-48 hours; first aid aims to save it.`,`3. Mediators raise capillary permeability, systemically in burns over 20-30%.`,`4. Plasma leak is maximal in the first 8-12 hours, causing burn shock.`,`5. Circumferential eschar acts as a tourniquet on limbs or chest.`,`6. After 48 hours, hypermetabolism doubles metabolic rate and slows healing.`,`7. Infection and sepsis are the main cause of late death.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Note the **time of injury**; the fluid clock starts at the burn.`,`Ask about an enclosed space, which suggests inhalation injury.`,`Ask about first aid given and home remedies that need washing off.`,`Check **tetanus** immunisation status and give toxoid if due.`,`Ask a woman with a kerosene burn, alone, about assault or dowry harassment.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look first for **inhalation injury**: facial burns, soot, hoarse voice, stridor.`,`Adults: use the **Wallace rule of nines**.`,`Children: use a **Lund and Browder chart**; an infant's head is about 18%.`,`The patient's own palm with fingers is about 1% TBSA.`,`Check circumferential burns hourly for perfusion and pain on passive stretch.`,`A clean tide-mark scald or glove-and-stocking pattern suggests non-accidental injury.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Send Hb, electrolytes, creatinine, glucose and cross-match in major burns.`,`Blood gas with **carboxyhaemoglobin** in every fire victim.`,`Normal SpO2 does **not** exclude carbon monoxide poisoning.`,`Lactate over 8 mmol/L after smoke inhalation suggests cyanide.`,`ECG, CK and urine myoglobin in electrical burns.`,`Hourly urine output is the main test that steers fluids.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Stop the burning; switch off electricity before touching the casualty.`,`**Cool running water for 20 minutes**, useful up to 3 hours after injury.`,`No ice, toothpaste, ink, turmeric, ghee or mud.`,`Cool the burn but **keep the patient warm**.`,`Cover with **cling film in strips**, never wrapped round.`,`Give 100% oxygen by non-rebreathing mask to every fire casualty.`,`Chemical burns: irrigate 20-30 minutes, 60 minutes or more for alkalis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`IV fluid for burns over 15% in adults or 10% in children.`,`**Parkland**: 4 mL x kg x %TBSA of Ringer lactate in 24 hours.`,`Give half in the **first 8 hours from the time of burn**.`,`ATLS now starts at 2 mL/kg/% adults, 3 children, 4 electrical.`,`Titrate to urine **0.5 mL/kg/h** in adults and 1 mL/kg/h in children under 30 kg.`,`Children also need maintenance dextrose fluid by the 4-2-1 rule.`,`Give IV morphine; no prophylactic antibiotics and no colloid in the first 8-12 hours.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Stridor, hoarseness or soot with distress means **early intubation** before transfer.`,`Succinylcholine is unsafe after about 48 hours because of hyperkalaemia.`,`Circumferential burn with rising pain or paraesthesia needs **escharotomy**.`,`Early hypotension after an isolated burn means another injury.`,`Dark urine means myoglobinuria; raise urine target to 1-1.5 mL/kg/h.`,`Fluid creep causes abdominal and limb compartment syndromes.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer partial thickness over 10%, any full thickness and special sites.`,`Special sites: face, hands, feet, genitalia, perineum and major joints.`,`Also refer electrical, chemical, inhalation, circumferential burns and suspected abuse.`,`A wound not healed by **2-3 weeks** is deep and needs grafting.`,`Burns in a woman within **7 years of marriage** need police intimation.`,`Use pressure garments and splints to prevent hypertrophic scars and contractures.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and fluids in burns`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ringer lactate`,`Parkland 4 mL/kg/%TBSA IV`,`Half in 8 h, rest over 16 h`,`Fluid creep, pulmonary oedema`],[`Morphine`,`0.1 mg/kg IV in 1-2 mg steps`,`As needed`,`Respiratory depression; IV not IM`],[`Silver sulfadiazine 1%`,`Topical, 3-5 mm layer`,`Once or twice daily`,`Avoid face, infants, G6PD, near term`],[`Tetanus toxoid and TIG`,`Td 0.5 mL IM; TIG 250 IU IM`,`Single dose`,`TIG if unimmunised or unknown`],[`Oxygen 100%`,`15 L/min non-rebreathing mask`,`Until COHb under 5%`,`Give regardless of SpO2`],[`Hydroxocobalamin`,`5 g IV over 15 min`,`Repeat once`,`Red skin and urine`],[`Pantoprazole`,`40 mg IV daily`,`Until enteral feeding`,`Curling ulcer prophylaxis`]]},{heading:`Burn depth`,columns:[`Depth`,`Look`,`Feel`,`Heals`],rows:[[`Superficial`,`Red, dry, no blisters`,`Painful, blanches`,`5-7 days, no scar`],[`Superficial partial`,`Pink, moist, blisters`,`Very painful, blanches`,`10-14 days`],[`Deep partial`,`Blotchy red-white`,`Reduced, sluggish blanching`,`Over 3 weeks, graft`],[`Full thickness`,`White, waxy, leathery`,`Insensate, no blanching`,`Edges only, graft`]]},{heading:`Rule of nines`,columns:[`Region`,`Adult %`,`Infant %`],rows:[[`Head and neck`,`9`,`18`],[`Each arm`,`9`,`9`],[`Front of trunk`,`18`,`18`],[`Back of trunk`,`18`,`18`],[`Each leg`,`18`,`14`],[`Perineum`,`1`,`1`]]}],remember:[`Cool the burn, warm the patient: 20 minutes of running water.`,`Erythema is not counted in TBSA.`,`Parkland 4 x kg x %TBSA; half in the first 8 hours from the burn.`,`Late arrival means faster fluid, not a later clock.`,`Urine output runs the drip: 0.5 adults, 1 mL/kg/h children.`,`Normal SpO2 does not exclude carbon monoxide.`,`Insensate, non-blanching, leathery skin is full thickness.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Burns`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Tissue injury from thermal, electrical, chemical, radiation or friction energy; India - **kerosene/LPG flame burns in women**, **scalds in children**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Depth**: superficial (not counted), superficial partial, deep partial, full thickness, fourth degree. **Extent**: %TBSA by **rule of nines** (adult), **Lund-Browder** (child), **palm 1%**. Major: **over 15% adult, over 10% child** or inhalation injury.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Kitchen fires, kerosene stoves, scalds, electrical (high voltage), chemicals (alkali worst, HF acid); enclosed-space fire (inhalation); extremes of age, epilepsy, alcohol; **dowry violence**, non-accidental injury.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Jackson zones: coagulation / **stasis (salvageable 24-48 h)** / hyperaemia -> mediators -> capillary leak (max **8-12 h**) -> burn shock with haemoconcentration -> hypermetabolism -> infection and sepsis (late death).`],[`Key history`,`**Time of burn** (clock starts here), mechanism, **enclosed space**, voltage/chemical, first aid given (home remedies), AMPLE, tetanus status, safeguarding (story fits?).`],[`Key examination`,`Airway: **facial burns, singed nasal hair, soot, hoarseness, stridor**; circumferential chest/limb burns; TBSA and depth (CRT, pin-prick); electrical entry/exit, ECG, dark urine; NAI patterns (tide-mark, glove-stocking).`],[`Investigations`,`Hb/haematocrit, electrolytes, creatinine, glucose, group and cross-match; **COHb by co-oximetry** (SpO2 falsely normal); lactate over 8 suggests cyanide; ECG, CK, urine myoglobin in electrical; **hourly urine output**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Stop burning; **cool running water 20 min (useful up to 3 h)**; no ice, toothpaste, ghee; **warm the patient**; cling film in strips; chemical - irrigate 20-30 min (alkali 60+ min); elevate.`],[`Treatment - fluids`,`IV Ringer lactate if over 15% adult / 10% child: **Parkland 4 mL/kg/%TBSA/24 h**, half in first 8 h **from time of burn**; ATLS/ABA start **2 adult, 3 child, 4 electrical**; titrate to urine **0.5 mL/kg/h (1 mL/kg/h child under 30 kg, 1-1.5 if myoglobin)**; children add maintenance dextrose.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Intubate early** for stridor, hoarseness, deep facial burns, GCS 8 or less, long transfer - largest uncut tube; succinylcholine unsafe after 48 h; **escharotomy** for circumferential burns (mid-medial/lateral lines; chest along anterior axillary lines).`],[`Treatment - drugs`,`IV morphine 0.1 mg/kg titrated (not IM); **100% O2** for CO; **hydroxocobalamin 5 g IV** for cyanide; Td + TIG; **no prophylactic systemic antibiotics**; SSD 1% (not face, near-term pregnancy, under 2 months, G6PD); PPI if over 20%; early enteral feed.`],[`Special groups`,`**Child**: Lund-Browder, 3 mL/kg/%, maintenance dextrose, urine 1 mL/kg/h, NAI. **Elderly**: high mortality (Baux). **Pregnancy**: O2 and HBO threshold COHb over 15%. **Electrical**: 4 mL/kg/%, cardiac monitor 24 h.`],[`Complications`,`Inhalation injury, CO/cyanide, **fluid creep** (abdominal/limb compartment syndrome), myoglobinuric AKI, Curling ulcer, wound sepsis, toxic shock in children, hypertrophic scar and contracture.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Burn-centre criteria**: partial thickness over 10%, any full thickness, face/hands/feet/genitalia/perineum/joints, circumferential, electrical, chemical, inhalation, comorbidity, trauma, suspected abuse.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Minor burns review at **48 h**; not healed by 2-3 weeks -> graft referral; pressure garments 23 h/day for 6-12 months, silicone, splints, physiotherapy; sun protection 1 year; PTSD screening.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NPPMBI**; safer stoves/LPG (Ujjwala); community cool-water first aid; married woman burnt **within 7 years of marriage** - police intimation and **dying declaration** verbatim.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - ATLS 11e starting rates (2/3/4 mL/kg/%) and ABA referral criteria remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Erythema is not counted; the 8-hour clock starts at the burn; early hypotension in an isolated burn means another injury; SpO2 is normal in CO poisoning.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Major burn: first hour`,caption:`Condensed exam version. [ATLS 11e, EMSB 2021]`,steps:[{label:`Stop the burning, cool 20 min`,detail:`Running water up to 3 h; no ice; keep patient warm`},{label:`Airway threatened?`,detail:`Soot, singed hair, hoarse voice, stridor, deep facial burn`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes: intubate early, 100% O2`,detail:`Largest uncut tube; consider CO and cyanide`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Estimate TBSA and depth`,detail:`Rule of nines / Lund-Browder / palm 1%; ignore erythema`},{label:`Over 15% adult or 10% child: IV RL`,detail:`Start 2 mL/kg/% adult (Parkland 4); half in 8 h from burn`},{label:`Catheter, titrate to urine output`,detail:`0.5 mL/kg/h adult, 1 mL/kg/h child; adjust rate by one-third`},{label:`Cling film, analgesia, transfer`,detail:`Written TBSA, fluid chart and time of burn`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Parkland against ATLS 11e starting rate`,caption:`Worked for a 60 kg adult with a 40% burn. [ATLS 11e]`,columns:[`Item`,`Parkland (classical)`,`ATLS 11e / ABA`],rows:[[`Formula`,`4 mL x kg x %TBSA`,`2 adult, 3 child, 4 electrical mL/kg/%`],[`24-hour volume`,`9600 mL`,`4800 mL`],[`First 8 h`,`4800 mL (600 mL/h)`,`2400 mL (300 mL/h)`],[`Next 16 h`,`4800 mL (300 mL/h)`,`2400 mL (150 mL/h)`],[`Steering`,`Urine output`,`Urine output; avoid fluid creep`]]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Burn depth at the bedside`,caption:`Sensation and blanching beat colour. [ISBI 2018]`,root:`Burn depth`,arms:[{label:`Heals by itself`,tone:`good`,steps:[`Superficial: red, dry, painful - 5-7 days`,`Superficial partial: blisters, moist, brisk blanching - 10-14 days`]},{label:`Needs surgery`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Deep partial: blotchy, sluggish blanching - over 3 weeks, graft`,`Full thickness: white/leathery, insensate - excise and graft`,`Fourth degree: fat, muscle, bone`]}]}]},"emergency-trauma-primary-survey":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`The primary survey finds and treats injuries **in the order they kill**.`,`Order is **xABCDE**: exsanguination, airway, breathing, circulation, disability, exposure.`,`Treat each problem as you find it, then move on.`,`Then come adjuncts, the secondary survey and transfer.`,`The survey targets the early death peak of the **golden hour**.`,`Good Samaritan law (2016) protects bystanders who help road accident victims.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**ATLS haemorrhage classes I-IV** link blood loss to the need for blood.`,`Class I is under 15% loss; class IV is over 40%.`,`BP usually falls only in class III, after 30% loss.`,`Six chest killers: airway obstruction, tension, open pneumothorax, massive haemothorax, flail chest, tamponade.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Haemorrhage is the commonest preventable cause of early trauma death.`,`2. Blood hides in five places: floor, chest, abdomen, pelvis and long bones.`,`3. Injury and shock trigger coagulopathy within minutes.`,`4. The **lethal diamond**: hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy and hypocalcaemia.`,`5. Excess crystalloid dilutes clotting factors and dislodges fresh clot.`,`6. In tension pneumothorax, rising pleural pressure kinks the vena cavae and stops venous return.`,`7. In head injury, hypoxia or hypotension each double mortality.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`x: stop exsanguinating limb bleeding with pressure or a **tourniquet**; write the time.`,`A: a **normal voice** means a patent airway; use jaw thrust with in-line stabilisation.`,`B: look, feel, percuss and listen; give oxygen 15 L/min.`,`C: **hypotension in trauma is haemorrhage** until proved otherwise.`,`D: GCS by components, pupils and capillary glucose.`,`E: undress fully, log-roll with four people, then cover and keep warm.`,`Reassess from A after every intervention or any deterioration.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Draw cross-match, Hb, venous gas with lactate and a pregnancy test at cannulation.`,`Chest and pelvic X-ray in the resuscitation room for blunt major trauma.`,`**eFAST** shows free fluid, pneumothorax and pericardial blood.`,`Never take an unstable patient to CT.`,`Tension pneumothorax is a **clinical diagnosis**, never an X-ray one.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Needle decompression: **4th or 5th intercostal space**, just anterior to mid-axillary line.`,`Follow needle decompression at once with a chest drain.`,`Open pneumothorax: occlusive dressing taped on three sides.`,`Pelvic binder at the **greater trochanters**, not the iliac crests.`,`Two large-bore cannulae; give 1 L warmed crystalloid, then assess response.`,`**Tranexamic acid 1 g IV** over 10 minutes within 3 hours of injury.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Transient or no response to fluid means bleeding; the next fluid is **blood**.`,`Give red cells, plasma and platelets in a **1:1:1** ratio.`,`Permissive hypotension: systolic **80-90 mmHg** until bleeding is controlled.`,`No permissive hypotension in head injury; keep systolic 110 mmHg or more.`,`Intubate for apnoea, GCS 8 or less, or hypoxia despite oxygen.`,`Open fracture: cefazolin 2 g IV within 1 hour.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Children compensate hard and then crash suddenly.`,`Children: decompress at the 2nd intercostal space, mid-clavicular line.`,`Pregnancy over 20 weeks: manual left uterine displacement and fetal heart check.`,`Elderly patients on beta-blockers cannot mount a tachycardia.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Blood at the meatus or perineal haematoma: **no urethral catheter**.`,`Base-of-skull fracture signs: no nasal tube or nasopharyngeal airway.`,`Sudden deterioration on a ventilator: think **DOPE**.`,`Fixed dilated pupil or GCS fall of 2 or more means herniation.`,`Never start tranexamic acid after 3 hours; it increases mortality.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Decide to transfer early on injuries and physiology, not test results.`,`Never transfer with untreated tension, uncontrolled bleeding or an unprotected airway.`,`Carry oxygen for twice the journey time with a trained escort.`,`Hand over using **ATMIST**, including the time TXA was given.`,`Road traffic injuries are medico-legal cases.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in trauma`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g IV over 10 min; child 15 mg/kg`,`Then 1 g over 8 h`,`Never start after 3 h`],[`Crystalloid then blood`,`1 L (child 20 mL/kg), then 1:1:1`,`By response`,`Dilution, hypothermia, low calcium`],[`Morphine`,`0.1 mg/kg IV in 1-2 mg steps`,`As needed`,`Respiratory depression; IV not IM`],[`Ketamine`,`0.1-0.3 mg/kg IV analgesia`,`Induction 1-2 mg/kg`,`Emergence reactions; keeps BP`],[`Cefazolin`,`2 g IV within 1 h`,`8-hourly for 24-72 h`,`Add gentamicin for Gustilo III`],[`Tetanus toxoid and TIG`,`Td 0.5 mL IM; TIG 250 IU IM`,`Single dose`,`Separate sites and syringes`],[`Mannitol 20%`,`0.25-1 g/kg IV over 15-20 min`,`For herniation signs`,`Diuresis, hypotension`]]},{heading:`ATLS haemorrhage classes`,columns:[`Class`,`Blood loss`,`Key signs`,`Blood needed`],rows:[[`I`,`Under 15%`,`All signs normal`,`Monitor`],[`II`,`15-30%`,`Narrow pulse pressure, anxious`,`Possible`],[`III`,`31-40%`,`Tachycardia, falling BP, confused`,`Yes`],[`IV`,`Over 40%`,`Hypotension, no urine, GCS falls`,`Massive transfusion`]]},{heading:`Chest killers at B and C`,columns:[`Injury`,`Clue`,`Immediate action`],rows:[[`Tension pneumothorax`,`Absent breath sounds, raised JVP, shock`,`Needle, then chest drain`],[`Open pneumothorax`,`Sucking chest wound`,`Three-sided dressing, drain`],[`Massive haemothorax`,`Dull, shocked; over 1500 mL drained`,`Drain, blood, thoracotomy`],[`Flail chest`,`Paradoxical segment, hypoxia`,`Oxygen, analgesia, ventilate`],[`Cardiac tamponade`,`Beck's triad, pulsus paradoxus`,`Pericardiocentesis, thoracotomy`]]}],remember:[`xABCDE: stop the bleeding before the airway.`,`A patient talking normally has an airway, breathing and brain perfusion.`,`Blood on the floor and four more: chest, abdomen, pelvis, long bones.`,`TXA within 3 hours, never after.`,`After 1 L crystalloid, the next fluid is blood.`,`Beck's triad: hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds.`,`Handover by ATMIST: age, time, mechanism, injuries, signs, treatment.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Trauma primary survey and safe transfer`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Rapid structured search that **treats life threats in the order they kill** - **xABCDE** - then adjuncts, secondary survey (head-to-toe + AMPLE), definitive care or transfer.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**ATLS haemorrhagic shock classes**: I under 15%, II 15-30% (narrow pulse pressure), III 31-40% (BP falls, transfuse), IV over 40% (MTP). Trimodal death - the primary survey targets the **early (golden hour) peak**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Road traffic injury (about 1.7 lakh deaths/year, two-wheelers, no helmet), falls, assault; anticoagulants, elderly on beta-blockers, pregnancy.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Haemorrhage -> compensated tachycardia until **30% loss** -> BP falls; **lethal diamond** (hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy, low Ca2+); tension -> kinked cavae -> obstructive shock; brain: hypoxia or SBP under 90 **doubles mortality**.`],[`Key history`,`**AMPLE** once stable: allergies, medications (anticoagulants, beta-blockers), past illness/pregnancy, last meal, events (speed, ejection, fall height, helmet); **time of injury** for TXA.`],[`Key examination`,`x: catastrophic bleed; A: talking = patent, jaw thrust + in-line stabilisation; B: RR, SpO2, trachea, breath sounds, percussion; C: 5 places blood hides, CRT, pulse pressure; D: **GCS E-V-M, pupils, glucose**; E: log-roll, warm.`],[`Investigations`,`Cross-match, Hb, **gas with lactate/base deficit**, glucose, pregnancy test; **CXR + pelvic X-ray** in resus; **eFAST**; CT only when stable; Canadian C-spine/NEXUS, NICE NG232 for head CT.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Direct pressure, **windlass tourniquet** (time written), pelvic binder at **greater trochanters**; O2 15 L/min; **2 large-bore cannulae** or IO; needle decompression **4th/5th ICS anterior to mid-axillary line** (child 2nd ICS MCL) then drain.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**1 L warm crystalloid** (child 20 mL/kg) -> response; transient/none -> **blood 1:1:1** + calcium; **TXA 1 g over 10 min within 3 h then 1 g over 8 h**; **permissive SBP 80-90** except head injury (SBP 110+); IV morphine/ketamine; cefazolin 2 g within 1 h for open fractures; Td/TIG.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Chest drain in **safe triangle** (5th ICS, anterior to mid-axillary line); massive haemothorax **1500 mL or 200 mL/h for 2-4 h** -> thoracotomy; three-sided dressing for open pneumothorax; traction splint for femur; pericardiocentesis as bridge.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy over 20 weeks**: manual left uterine displacement, resuscitate mother first, fetal heart. **Child**: compensates then crashes, 20 mL/kg. **Elderly / beta-blocked**: no tachycardia. **Anticoagulated**: PCC 25-50 IU/kg + vitamin K.`],[`Complications`,`Trauma coagulopathy, hypothermia, missed injuries, compartment syndrome, abdominal compartment, ARDS, sepsis, PTSD, chronic pain.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Absent breath sounds + raised JVP + hypotension (tension); deterioration on ventilator (**DOPE**); transient response to 1 L; blood at meatus (no catheter); fixed dilated pupil; cold oozing patient; no TXA after 3 h.`],[`Referral - safe transfer`,`Decide early on physiology not tests; doctor-to-doctor call; never transfer untreated tension, uncontrolled bleed, unprotected airway, unbound pelvis; O2 for **twice journey time**; trained escort; handover **ATMIST**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Reassess from A after every intervention; SpO2, ECG, BP every 5 min, ETCO2 if intubated; urine **0.5 mL/kg/h (child 1)**; **tertiary survey at 24 h** for missed injuries; compartments; then PTSD, chronic pain, return to work; helmet and seat-belt counselling.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Helmets, seat belts, no drink-driving; **Good Samaritan law (MoRTH 2016)**; **Cashless Treatment of Road Accident Victims Scheme 2025** (up to Rs 1.5 lakh for 7 days); medico-legal register.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - ATLS 11e (xABCDE, 4th/5th ICS needle site) and CRASH-2/3 TXA timing remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Treat as you find; talking patient = A, B and brain perfusion intact; after 1 L the next fluid is blood; binder at trochanters; tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`xABCDE: treat as you find`,caption:`Condensed exam version; reassess from A after every intervention. [ATLS 11e]`,steps:[{label:`x - Exsanguinating haemorrhage`,detail:`Pressure, windlass tourniquet (time it), pack junctional wounds`,tone:`warn`},{label:`A - Airway + cervical spine`,detail:`Jaw thrust, suction, in-line stabilisation; intubate if GCS 8 or less`},{label:`B - Breathing`,detail:`O2 15 L/min; tension -> needle 4th/5th ICS anterior to MAL, then drain`},{label:`C - Circulation`,detail:`2 large-bore lines, binder, 1 L warm crystalloid, TXA within 3 h`},{label:`Response to 1 L?`,detail:`Rapid / transient / none`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Transient or none: blood 1:1:1, surgery`,detail:`Permissive SBP 80-90 except head injury`,tone:`warn`},{label:`D - GCS, pupils, glucose; E - expose, warm`,detail:`Log-roll; then AMPLE and secondary survey`},{label:`Early transfer with ATMIST handover`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Six lethal chest injuries at B and C`,caption:`Find and treat in the primary survey. [ATLS 11e]`,columns:[`Injury`,`Clue`,`Immediate action`],rows:[[`Airway obstruction`,`Stridor, gurgling, no voice`,`Jaw thrust, suction, adjunct, surgical airway`],[`Tension pneumothorax`,`Absent breath sounds, raised JVP, shock`,`Needle 4th/5th ICS anterior to MAL, then drain`],[`Open pneumothorax`,`Sucking wound`,`Three-sided dressing, drain away from wound`],[`Massive haemothorax`,`Dull, absent breath sounds, 1500 mL drained`,`Drain, blood, thoracotomy`],[`Flail chest + contusion`,`Paradoxical segment, hypoxia`,`O2, analgesia, ventilate if hypoxic`],[`Cardiac tamponade`,`Beck triad, pericardial fluid on FAST`,`Pericardiocentesis as bridge, thoracotomy`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Haemorrhagic shock classes to blood`,caption:`Class sets the need for blood. [ATLS 11e]`,steps:[{label:`Class I: under 15%`,detail:`Vitals normal - monitor`},{label:`Class II: 15-30%`,detail:`Pulse pressure narrows, anxious - crystalloid, blood possible`},{label:`Class III: 31-40%`,detail:`BP falls, confused, oliguria - transfuse`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Class IV: over 40%`,detail:`Negligible urine, GCS down - massive transfusion 1:1:1 + TXA`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"emergency-altered-sensorium":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Altered sensorium is an acute disturbance of **arousal or content** of consciousness.`,`It is a symptom, never a diagnosis.`,`Arousal comes from the reticular activating system; content from the cortex.`,`**Delirium** is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention from a medical cause.`,`Hypoactive delirium is common and commonly missed.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Record the **Glasgow Coma Scale** as E, V and M separately; maximum 15.`,`GCS 13-15 is mild, 9-12 moderate, 3-8 severe.`,`**GCS 8 or less** means an unprotected airway.`,`AVPU score of P or U equals a GCS of about 8 or less.`,`Causes are **structural** (focal signs) or **metabolic and toxic** (diffuse, symmetric).`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Use **AEIOU TIPS** to work through causes at the bedside.`,`Hypoglycaemia from insulin or sulfonylurea is the commonest reversible cause.`,`In endemic areas think cerebral malaria, Japanese encephalitis, scrub typhus and dengue.`,`In the elderly look for new drugs, infection, urine retention and constipation.`,`Never accept alcohol or psychiatric illness as the label at first contact.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Coma needs both hemispheres affected or a brainstem reticular system lesion.`,`2. Metabolic causes depress neurons diffusely, so **pupil reflexes are preserved**.`,`3. The brain has almost no glucose stores, so hypoglycaemia quickly causes coma.`,`4. Glucose without thiamine in a deficient patient precipitates **Wernicke encephalopathy**.`,`5. Rising ICP lowers cerebral perfusion pressure (MAP minus ICP).`,`6. Uncal herniation compresses the third nerve, giving a fixed dilated pupil.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the relative what drugs, alcohol or poison were available.`,`Ask about diabetes, epilepsy, liver, kidney and psychiatric illness.`,`Ask what happened in the last 24 hours: headache, fever, fall or seizure.`,`Sudden onset suggests stroke, SAH or seizure; days suggest infection or metabolic.`,`Sulfonylurea hypoglycaemia can relapse for **24-72 hours**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check **capillary glucose in every patient**; no sign separates it from stroke.`,`The **motor score** carries most prognostic weight.`,`A unilateral fixed dilated pupil means herniation until proved otherwise.`,`Pinpoint pupils suggest opioid, organophosphate or pontine bleed.`,`Meningism may be absent in infants, the elderly and deep coma.`,`Chart GCS, pupils and vitals every 15 minutes with clock times.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Sodium, calcium, urea, creatinine, LFT, CBC and blood gas.`,`Malaria test, dengue test and blood cultures in febrile patients.`,`**CT head** for focal signs, trauma, sudden headache or unexplained coma.`,`Lumbar puncture for meningitis, but never delay antibiotics for it.`,`EEG if non-convulsive status epilepticus is suspected.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Protect the airway; recovery position or intubation if GCS 8 or less.`,`Give oxygen to keep SpO2 at 94-98%.`,`Hypoglycaemia: **25% dextrose 100 mL IV**; child 10% dextrose 2-5 mL/kg.`,`Give **thiamine** before or with glucose in malnourished or alcoholic patients.`,`Opioid toxicity: **naloxone 0.4 mg IV**, titrated to breathing, not wakefulness.`,`Seizure of 5 minutes or more: lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IV or midazolam 10 mg IM.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`After two benzodiazepine doses, give **levetiracetam 60 mg/kg IV** (max 4500 mg).`,`Suspected meningitis: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV** plus dexamethasone, before CT and LP.`,`Cerebral malaria: **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg** at 0, 12 and 24 hours.`,`Suspected HSV encephalitis: aciclovir 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly.`,`Severe hyponatraemia: 3% saline 100-150 mL; correct under 8-10 mmol/L in 24 hours.`,`Hepatic encephalopathy: lactulose to 2-3 soft stools daily.`,`Flumazenil is not given routinely; it provokes seizures.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer anyone not fully recovered, GCS 8 or less, or with focal signs.`,`Give first-dose antibiotic or antimalarial before transfer.`,`Send the timed GCS chart and every drug with dose and time.`,`Never send an unconscious patient in a private car with only a relative.`,`Describe alcohol as an odour, never certify it.`,`Stop or switch the sulfonylurea after severe hypoglycaemia.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in altered sensorium`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Dextrose`,`25% 100 mL IV; child 10% 2-5 mL/kg`,`Recheck at 15 min`,`Phlebitis, rebound hypoglycaemia`],[`Thiamine`,`200-500 mg IV; Wernicke 500 mg TDS`,`Wernicke: 2-3 days, then 250 mg daily`,`Rare anaphylaxis`],[`Naloxone`,`0.4 mg IV every 2-3 min`,`Up to about 10 mg; infusion`,`Withdrawal; wears off in 30-90 min`],[`Lorazepam or midazolam`,`Lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IV; midazolam 10 mg IM`,`Repeat once`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Levetiracetam`,`60 mg/kg IV over 10 min (max 4500 mg)`,`Single loading dose`,`Somnolence`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV 12-hourly`,`10-14 days`,`Allergy; give before LP`],[`Dexamethasone`,`10 mg IV 6-hourly`,`4 days`,`Hyperglycaemia, GI bleed`],[`Artesunate`,`2.4 mg/kg IV at 0, 12, 24 h, then daily`,`Until oral ACT possible`,`Delayed haemolysis`]]},{heading:`AEIOU TIPS`,columns:[`Letter`,`Causes`,`First action`],rows:[[`A`,`Alcohol, acidosis (DKA)`,`Glucose, thiamine, ketones`],[`E`,`Epilepsy, endocrine, electrolytes`,`Benzodiazepine; 3% saline if needed`],[`I`,`Insulin (hypoglycaemia)`,`25% dextrose 100 mL IV`],[`O`,`Opioids, oxygen lack`,`Naloxone; oxygen and ventilation`],[`U`,`Uraemia, hepatic encephalopathy`,`Dialysis referral; lactulose`],[`T`,`Trauma, temperature`,`Spine care, CT; cool or warm`],[`I`,`Infection`,`Ceftriaxone, dexamethasone; artesunate`],[`P`,`Poisoning, psychiatric`,`Antidote; psychiatric only after exclusion`],[`S`,`Stroke, SAH, seizures, shock, snake bite`,`CT head, neurosurgery; ASV`]]},{heading:`Glasgow Coma Scale`,columns:[`Component`,`Best score`,`Lowest score`],rows:[[`Eye opening (E)`,`4 spontaneous`,`1 none`],[`Verbal (V)`,`5 oriented`,`1 none`],[`Motor (M)`,`6 obeys commands`,`1 none`],[`Total`,`15`,`3`]]}],remember:[`Glucose first, every time; it takes 30 seconds.`,`Thiamine before or with dextrose in the malnourished or alcoholic.`,`GCS 8 or less: intubate.`,`Metabolic coma spares pupils; a fixed unilateral pupil means structure.`,`AEIOU TIPS for causes.`,`Antibiotics for meningitis go in before CT and LP.`,`Elderly delirium: check drugs, bladder, bowel and chest.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Altered sensorium`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute disturbance of **arousal (ARAS)** or **content (cortex)** of consciousness - a symptom, never a diagnosis; levels: delirium, drowsiness, stupor, coma (GCS 8 or less).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**GCS E4 V5 M6** recorded by component: mild 13-15, moderate 9-12, severe 3-8; **AVPU** (P/U about GCS 8); causes - **structural (focal) vs metabolic/toxic/infective (symmetric)** by **AEIOU TIPS**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Hypoglycaemia, hypoxia, sepsis, meningitis/encephalitis, **cerebral malaria**, JE, scrub typhus, stroke/SAH, trauma, poisoning, alcohol/Wernicke, uraemia, hepatic encephalopathy, hyponatraemia, status epilepticus, heat stroke.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Coma needs **bilateral hemisphere dysfunction or ARAS lesion**; mass -> ICP rise -> CPP = MAP - ICP falls -> uncal herniation (CN III, fixed dilated pupil) -> Cushing response; metabolic coma spares pupillary reflexes.`],[`Key history`,`Three questions: drugs/alcohol/poison available, illnesses (diabetes on SU, epilepsy, liver, kidney), last 24 h (headache, fever, fall, seizure); tempo of onset; travel/monsoon; elderly - new drugs, retention.`],[`Key examination`,`**GCS by components every 15 min**, pupils, doll's eye, lateralising signs, meningism (absent in infants/elderly/deep coma), fundi, rash/eschar, breath odour, toxidrome, bladder, **temperature**.`],[`Investigations`,`**Capillary glucose in every patient**; Na, K, Ca, urea, creatinine, LFT, CBC; gas and ketones; malaria RDT/smear, dengue, blood cultures, scrub IgM; **CT head** if focal/trauma/papilloedema/anticoagulated; LP after CT - never delay antibiotics; EEG for NCSE.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Airway (recovery position, suction, intubate if GCS 8 or less), O2 94-98%, correct the **four now** (hypoglycaemia, hypoxia, hypotension, temperature), head up 30 degrees, catheter, eye and pressure care; avoid sedation until glucose/hypoxia excluded.`],[`Treatment - bedside cocktail`,`**25% dextrose 100 mL IV** (child 10% 2-5 mL/kg); **thiamine 200-500 mg IV** (Wernicke 500 mg TDS); **naloxone 0.4 mg** titrated to RR; status: **lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg (max 4 mg)** or midazolam 10 mg IM (buccal 0.5 mg/kg, intranasal 0.2 mg/kg; max 10 mg), then **levetiracetam 60 mg/kg (max 4.5 g)**.`],[`Treatment - infection`,`Meningitis: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly** + **dexamethasone 10 mg 6-hourly x 4 days** before LP/transfer (+ ampicillin if over 60/pregnant); HSV: **aciclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly**; cerebral malaria: **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h** (3 mg/kg under 20 kg) or rectal pre-referral.`],[`Treatment - metabolic causes`,`Hepatic: lactulose to 2-3 stools + rifaximin 550 mg BD; hyponatraemia with seizures: **3% saline 100-150 mL over 10-20 min**, max rise **8-10 mmol/L in 24 h**; hypertensive encephalopathy: labetalol, MAP down **20-25% in first hour**; eclampsia: MgSO4.`],[`Special groups`,`**Child**: 10% dextrose 2-5 mL/kg, IAP; inconsistent story - inflicted injury. **Elderly**: hypoactive delirium, drugs, retention, constipation; haloperidol 0.5-1 mg only if essential. **Pregnancy/postpartum**: eclampsia until proved otherwise.`],[`Complications`,`Aspiration, herniation, status, osmotic demyelination with fast Na correction, Wernicke-Korsakoff, recurrent SU hypoglycaemia (24-72 h), pressure sores.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`GCS 8 or less or fall of 2; unilateral fixed pupil, Cushing; fever + neck stiffness/purpura; seizure after 2 benzodiazepines; malaria-endemic fever with coma; alcohol smell + head injury (subdural).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Transfer with trained escort, timed GCS chart, drugs given; review SU after severe hypoglycaemia, epilepsy adherence, alcohol de-addiction, deprescribing; delirium survivors - cognitive screening.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - ADA 2026 hypoglycaemia, NICE NG240 meningitis, AES 2016/ESETT status epilepticus and WHO artesunate dosing remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Glucose first, every time; thiamine with glucose in the malnourished; naloxone to breathing, not wakefulness; antibiotics before CT and LP; alcohol is an odour, not a diagnosis.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Unconscious patient: first 5 minutes`,caption:`Condensed exam version. [Harrison 22e, NICE NG240 2024]`,steps:[{label:`Airway, recovery position, O2`,detail:`Spinal precautions if trauma possible; intubate if GCS 8 or less`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Capillary glucose`,detail:`Under 70 mg/dL: 25% dextrose 100 mL IV + thiamine if malnourished`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Pinpoint pupils, RR under 12?`,detail:`Naloxone 0.4 mg IV titrated to breathing`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Seizing 5 minutes or more?`,detail:`Lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg, repeat once, then levetiracetam 60 mg/kg`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Fever, neck stiffness or rash?`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 2 g + dexamethasone; artesunate if malaria-endemic`,tone:`decision`},{label:`GCS by components, pupils, focal signs`,detail:`Focal or trauma -> CT head; work through AEIOU TIPS`},{label:`Transfer with escort and timed chart`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Convulsive status epilepticus ladder`,caption:`Time-based escalation. [AES 2016, ESETT]`,steps:[{label:`0-5 min: ABC, glucose`,detail:`Recovery position, O2, IV access`},{label:`5 min: benzodiazepine`,detail:`Lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IV (max 4 mg) or midazolam 10 mg IM, 0.5 mg/kg buccal or 0.2 mg/kg intranasal`},{label:`10 min: repeat benzodiazepine once`,detail:`Maximum two doses`},{label:`20 min: second-line`,detail:`Levetiracetam 60 mg/kg (max 4.5 g) or valproate 40 mg/kg or fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg`},{label:`40 min: refractory - ICU`,detail:`Intubate; midazolam, propofol or thiopentone infusion`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Structural against metabolic coma`,caption:`The pupils and symmetry sort the cause. [Adams and Victor 12e]`,root:`Coma`,arms:[{label:`Structural`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Focal or asymmetric signs`,`Unilateral fixed dilated pupil`,`Cushing response`,`CT head and neurosurgery`]},{label:`Metabolic / toxic / infective`,tone:`neutral`,steps:[`Symmetric signs`,`Pupillary reflexes preserved`,`Glucose, Na, gas, malaria, cultures`,`Treat cause; LP for meningitis`]}]}]},"emergency-tray-and-readiness":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`The emergency tray is one **sealed, checklist-controlled** set of drugs and equipment.`,`It lets any clinic treat the first minutes of an emergency without searching.`,`Keep it in **one fixed, unlocked place**, reachable within 30 seconds.`,`An item belongs on the tray only if it treats a condition that **kills within minutes**.`,`Readiness has three parts: the tray, the people and the referral pathway.`,`AEFI rules demand an anaphylaxis kit at every immunisation session.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Manchester Triage System** has five colours with a maximum time to doctor.`,`Red is immediate, orange 10 minutes, yellow 60 minutes.`,`Green is 120 minutes and blue 240 minutes.`,`Indian emergency departments use a three-colour red-yellow-green triage.`,`NABH and IPHS 2022 require documented emergency drugs, equipment and trained staff.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Irreversible brain injury begins **4-6 minutes** after circulatory arrest.`,`2. Survival in VF falls **7-10% per minute** without defibrillation.`,`3. Anaphylaxis after an injected trigger can arrest in about **5 minutes**.`,`4. Seizures beyond 5 minutes rarely stop alone, so benzodiazepine works best early.`,`5. Heat and light turn adrenaline into inactive pink-brown adrenochrome.`,`6. Under stress working memory fails, so roles and charts must be pre-decided.`]},{title:`Organising the tray`,points:[`Seal it with a **numbered breakable tag**.`,`Arrange items **by indication**, not alphabetically.`,`Keep **1:1000 and 1:10,000 adrenaline** physically separate and clearly labelled.`,`Tape a laminated weight-based paediatric dose chart inside the lid.`,`Never store emergency drugs in the vaccine refrigerator.`,`Move items within **3 months of expiry** to routine use and replace them.`,`Match the tray to the practice: extra atropine in the pesticide belt.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Anaphylaxis: **adrenaline 1:1000 IM thigh**, 0.5 mg adult, repeat every 5 minutes.`,`Cardiac arrest: **adrenaline 1 mg IV of 1:10,000** every 3-5 minutes.`,`Giving 1:1000 IV as if it were 1:10,000 is a **ten-fold** lethal error.`,`Hypoglycaemia: 25% dextrose 100 mL IV; child 10% dextrose 2-5 mL/kg.`,`Seizure at 5 minutes: lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IV or midazolam 10 mg IM.`,`Opioid respiratory depression: naloxone 0.4 mg IV or IM.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Bradycardia: atropine **1 mg IV** every 3-5 minutes, maximum 3 mg.`,`Indian atropine ampoules are usually 0.6 mg/mL; read the strength.`,`Suspected MI: aspirin 300 mg chewed plus clopidogrel 300 mg.`,`Eclampsia: **magnesium sulphate** 4 g IV plus 5 g IM in each buttock.`,`Postpartum haemorrhage: oxytocin 10 IU IM and **tranexamic acid 1 g IV** within 3 hours.`,`Hydrocortisone is an adjunct, never instead of adrenaline.`]},{title:`Devices`,points:[`Oxygen cylinder with a spare, regulator and **key attached**; log pressure weekly.`,`Bag-valve-mask **with reservoir** gives about 90% oxygen; without it, about 50%.`,`Carry adult, paediatric and neonatal bags with masks sizes 0-5.`,`Size the oropharyngeal airway from incisors to angle of jaw; remove if the patient gags.`,`Suction must be **manual or foot-operated** to work in a power cut.`,`AED, glucometer with in-date strips, cannulae 14G-24G and an intraosseous needle.`]},{title:`Weekly check and mock drill`,points:[`One **named owner** checks weekly on a fixed day and signs the log.`,`Check adrenaline first: present, correct strength, in date, not discoloured.`,`Test suction with the mains off and run the AED self-test.`,`**Restock and reseal after every use** before the next patient.`,`Run an **unannounced mock drill every 3 months** with fixed roles.`,`Time first compression, adrenaline drawn up and ambulance called.`,`Renew BLS for every staff member every 2 years.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Immunisation: anaphylaxis kit at the table and **30-minute observation** for every vaccinee.`,`Vasovagal faint has bradycardia and pallor; anaphylaxis has tachycardia and urticaria.`,`Delivery point: oxytocin in cold chain, magnesium sulphate, calcium gluconate, TXA.`,`Regional blocks: keep **20% lipid emulsion** for local anaesthetic toxicity.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Post a written referral pathway with the hospital number and 108.`,`Audit weekly checks signed, target 100%.`,`Audit expired or missing items at unannounced checks, target zero.`,`A tray without a signed, dated log is presumed unchecked.`,`Report every AEFI and review each emergency within a week.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs on the emergency tray`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Adrenaline 1:1000`,`0.5 mg IM thigh (0.3 mg 6-12 y; 0.15 mg 6 m-6 y)`,`Repeat every 5 min`,`Never IV outside monitoring`],[`Adrenaline 1:10,000`,`1 mg IV/IO; child 0.01 mg/kg`,`Every 3-5 min in arrest`,`Store apart from 1:1000`],[`Atropine`,`1 mg IV (max 3 mg); OP 1.8-3 mg doubled`,`Bradycardia; OP to dry chest`,`Ampoules usually 0.6 mg/mL`],[`Dextrose`,`25% 100 mL IV; child 10% 2-5 mL/kg`,`Recheck at 15 min`,`Thiamine first if alcoholic`],[`Lorazepam or midazolam`,`0.1 mg/kg IV; midazolam 10 mg IM`,`Maximum two doses`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Naloxone`,`0.4 mg IV or IM`,`Repeat every 2-3 min`,`Shorter acting than most opioids`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`4 g IV plus 10 g IM, then 5 g IM 4-hourly`,`24 h after last fit`,`Check reflexes, RR, urine`],[`Oxytocin and TXA`,`Oxytocin 10 IU IM; TXA 1 g IV`,`TXA repeat once at 30 min`,`TXA never after 3 h`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`100-200 mg IV; child 4 mg/kg`,`6-hourly if continued`,`Adjunct only`],[`Aspirin and clopidogrel`,`Aspirin 300 mg chewed; clopidogrel 300 mg`,`Single loading dose`,`Clopidogrel 75 mg if over 75`]]},{heading:`Manchester Triage System`,columns:[`Colour`,`Name`,`Max time to doctor`,`Example`],rows:[[`Red`,`Immediate`,`0 min`,`Airway compromise, shock, seizure`],[`Orange`,`Very urgent`,`10 min`,`Cardiac chest pain`],[`Yellow`,`Urgent`,`60 min`,`Persistent vomiting`],[`Green`,`Standard`,`120 min`,`Minor injury`],[`Blue`,`Non-urgent`,`240 min`,`Chronic problem`]]},{heading:`Devices and common errors`,columns:[`Device`,`Key step`,`Common error`],rows:[[`Oxygen cylinder`,`Key attached, 15 L/min, log pressure`,`Empty cylinder, missing key`],[`Bag-valve-mask`,`Reservoir on, C-E grip, 1 s squeeze`,`No reservoir, adult bag on infant`],[`Oropharyngeal airway`,`Size incisors to jaw angle`,`Forcing it when patient gags`],[`Suction`,`Yankauer under vision, 10-15 s`,`Electric-only, fails in power cut`],[`AED`,`Pads on, clear, shock, resume CPR`,`Long pauses, delayed first shock`],[`Glucometer`,`In-date strips, weekly control`,`Expired strips`]]}],remember:[`Adrenaline is the one indefensible omission: check it first, and its concentration.`,`Anaphylaxis 1:1000 IM thigh: 0.5, 0.3, 0.15 mg by age band.`,`Arrest 1 mg IV of 1:10,000; 1:1000 IV is a ten-fold error.`,`A bag without its reservoir gives 50% oxygen, not 90%.`,`Arrange by emergency, laminate the child chart, seal with a numbered tag.`,`The commonest empty tray is the one just used.`,`Time the drill, not the discussion.`]},updated:[`GINA 2026: oxygen in acute asthma only if SpO2 under 92%, titrated to no higher than 95%; oral prednisolone preferred, IV hydrocortisone if unable to swallow`,`ESC 2026: acute heart failure now termed decompensated heart failure, with early urine sodium-guided diuretic escalation`,`ERC 2025: airway adjunct technique tag updated (insertion unchanged)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Emergency tray and clinic readiness`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**One sealed, checklist-controlled tray in one fixed, unlocked place, reachable in 30 s**, holding only what treats conditions that **kill within minutes**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Readiness = **tray + people + pathway**. Triage: **Manchester (red 0, orange 10, yellow 60, green 120, blue 240 min)**; Indian EDs use 3-colour red-yellow-green.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`NABH entry-level/SHCO standards (documented drugs, equipment, trained staff, referral); **IPHS 2022** PHC/AAM drug list; **AEFI 2024** - anaphylaxis kit at every session + **30-min observation**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Brain injury after **4-6 min** of arrest; VF survival falls 7-10%/min without shock; iatrogenic anaphylaxis arrests in about **5 min**; status beyond 5 min self-perpetuates; adrenaline degrades in heat and light.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Adrenaline 1:1000 IM 0.5/0.3/0.15 mg** by age, repeat 5 min; **adrenaline 1 mg IV (1:10,000)** every 3-5 min in arrest; amiodarone 300/150 mg; **atropine 1 mg IV every 3-5 min to 3 mg** for bradycardia (Indian ampoule 0.6 mg/mL).`],[`Treatment - metabolic and neuro`,`**25% dextrose 100 mL** (child 10% 2-5 mL/kg) + thiamine 100 mg; glucagon 1 mg IM; **lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg** or midazolam 10 mg IM / buccal 0.5 mg/kg / intranasal 0.2 mg/kg (max 10 mg); **naloxone 0.4 mg** to 10 mg.`],[`Treatment - respiratory and cardiac`,`Salbutamol 5 mg neb or 4-10 puffs by spacer every 20 min + ipratropium 500 microgram; **O2 only if SpO2 under 92%, target up to 95%**; prednisolone 40-50 mg (hydrocortisone 100-200 mg IV if cannot swallow); aspirin 300 mg + clopidogrel 300 mg; GTN; furosemide 40 mg IV.`],[`Treatment - obstetric and others`,`**MgSO4 4 g IV + 10 g IM (Pritchard)**, then 5 g IM 4-hourly; calcium gluconate 10 mL antidote; **oxytocin 10 IU IM**; **TXA 1 g IV within 3 h**; NS/RL, ORS; lipid 20% 1.5 mL/kg where blocks are done; atropine 25+ ampoules in pesticide belt.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`O2 cylinder with key, NRB mask 15 L/min (reservoir pre-filled); **BVM with reservoir (90% vs 50%)** adult 1500, child 500, neonate 240 mL; C-E grip; OPA sized incisor-angle, rotate 180 in adults; **manual/foot suction**; AED (paediatric pads under 8 y); glucometer with control.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Arrange **by indication, not alphabet**; separate and label **1:1000 vs 1:10,000**; laminated paediatric chart in lid; numbered seal; never in vaccine fridge; **named owner**, weekly signed check, **first-expiry-first-out**, out at 3 months to expiry.`],[`Special groups`,`**Immunisation**: adrenaline ampoules, 1 mL syringes, infant masks, dose card, 30-min observation; **delivery point**: oxytocin (cold chain), MgSO4, calcium, TXA, neonatal bag; procedure room: separated LA with/without adrenaline; home-visit bag.`],[`Prevention - mock drill`,`**Unannounced mock drill every 3 months** (anaphylaxis after injection, collapse, fitting child, PPH); 6 fixed roles (compressions, tray/AED, drugs, phone, family, scribe); time first compression, adrenaline drawn, ambulance call; blame-free debrief.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Adrenaline missing/expired/discoloured; 1:1000 and 1:10,000 stored together; tray locked; empty O2 or no key; BVM without reservoir; log gaps or back-dated; no paediatric chart; tray not restocked.`],[`Follow-up & audit indicators`,`Indicators: weekly checks completed (100%), expired/missing at **unannounced** check (zero), drill times trending down, BLS currency (every 2 years for all staff), complete AEFI kits; report AEFI and review every emergency.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**GINA 2026**: O2 only below 92%, upper target 95%; **ESC 2026**: 'decompensated heart failure', urine sodium-guided diuresis; AHA 2025 adult bradycardia atropine **1 mg** to 3 mg.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Adrenaline is the one indefensible omission - check it first; 1:1000 IV is a ten-fold error; the commonest empty tray is the one just used; faint = bradycardia and pallor, anaphylaxis = tachycardia and urticaria.`]]},flows:[{kind:`cycle`,heading:`The tray readiness cycle`,caption:`Ownership turns a list into a working tray. [NABH 2020]`,steps:[{label:`Weekly check by named owner`,detail:`Seal number, drugs present, in date, not discoloured; adrenaline first`},{label:`Test the devices`,detail:`O2 pressure, BVM valves, suction with mains off, AED self-test, glucometer control`},{label:`Rotate and reseal`,detail:`First-expiry-first-out; out at 3 months to expiry; new numbered tag; sign log`},{label:`Use in emergency`,detail:`Timed record sheet, fixed roles`},{label:`Restock before next patient`,detail:`The commonest empty tray is the one just used`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Quarterly unannounced drill and audit`,detail:`Time first compression, adrenaline drawn, ambulance call; debrief`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Adrenaline 1:1000 against 1:10,000`,caption:`The ten-fold error that kills. [RCUK 2021, AHA 2025]`,columns:[`Item`,`1:1000 (1 mg/mL)`,`1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL)`],rows:[[`Use`,`Anaphylaxis`,`Cardiac arrest`],[`Route`,`IM anterolateral thigh`,`IV / IO`],[`Adult dose`,`0.5 mg (0.5 mL)`,`1 mg (10 mL) every 3-5 min`],[`Child dose`,`0.3 mg 6-12 y; 0.15 mg 6 m-6 y`,`0.01 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg), max 1 mg`],[`Never`,`IV bolus outside monitoring`,`IM for anaphylaxis`]]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Atropine: bradycardia against organophosphate`,caption:`Same ampoule, very different dose. [AHA 2025, Eddleston 2008]`,columns:[`Item`,`Symptomatic bradycardia`,`Organophosphate poisoning`],rows:[[`Adult dose`,`1 mg IV every 3-5 min`,`1.8-3 mg IV, doubled every 5 min`],[`Maximum`,`3 mg`,`No maximum (often 50-100 mg+)`],[`End-point`,`Heart rate and perfusion`,`Clear chest, HR over 80, SBP over 80, dry axillae`],[`Child`,`0.02 mg/kg, max single 0.5 mg`,`0.02-0.05 mg/kg doubling`],[`Stock`,`Few ampoules`,`25 or more ampoules in pesticide belt`]]}]},"emergency-ards":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`ARDS is acute, diffuse, inflammatory **permeability** lung injury.`,`Berlin 2012 needs all four: timing, imaging, origin of oedema, oxygenation.`,`**Timing**: within 1 week of a known insult or new symptoms.`,`**Imaging**: bilateral opacities not explained by effusion, collapse or nodules.`,`**Origin**: not fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload.`,`**Oxygenation**: PaO2/FiO2 measured on PEEP or CPAP of at least 5 cmH2O.`,`Clinicians recognise only about 60% of cases.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Mild ARDS has a PaO2/FiO2 ratio of **201-300**.`,`Moderate ARDS has a PaO2/FiO2 ratio of **101-200**.`,`Severe ARDS has a PaO2/FiO2 ratio of **100 or less**.`,`Example: PaO2 60 on 60% oxygen is 60 divided by 0.6, which is 100: severe.`,`Berlin abolished 'acute lung injury' and the wedge pressure cut-off.`,`2023 global definition adds high-flow oxygen, SpO2/FiO2 315 or less and lung ultrasound.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Sepsis** is the commonest cause overall, about 40%.`,`**Pneumonia** is the commonest direct (pulmonary) cause.`,`Other direct causes: aspiration, inhalation, contusion, drowning, fat embolism.`,`Indirect causes: trauma with shock, transfusion, pancreatitis, burns, eclampsia.`,`Indian precipitants: falciparum malaria, dengue, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, organophosphate, paraquat.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The insult activates macrophages, which release IL-1, IL-6, IL-8 and TNF.`,`2. Neutrophils damage the alveolar-capillary membrane on both sides.`,`3. Protein-rich exudate floods the alveoli and **hyaline membranes** form.`,`4. Surfactant loss collapses alveoli, creating a **shunt** that resists added oxygen.`,`5. The aerated 'baby lung' is small, so normal tidal volumes overdistend it.`,`6. Repair follows over 7-21 days; a minority progress to fibrosis.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Breathlessness worsening over hours to days after an insult.`,`Ask about fever, sepsis, vomiting with aspiration, trauma, pancreatitis and transfusion.`,`Ask about monsoon fever, pesticide ingestion and snakebite.`,`Prior orthopnoea, cardiac disease or missed dialysis favour cardiogenic oedema.`,`Inability to speak in sentences or drowsiness means exhaustion.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Respiratory rate above 30, accessory muscles and paradoxical breathing.`,`SpO2 that stays low on 15 L/min oxygen means **true shunt**.`,`Normal JVP, no S3 and no oedema argue against cardiogenic oedema.`,`A normal or rising PaCO2 in an exhausted patient is pre-terminal.`,`Hunt for the source: abdomen, skin, eschar, fractures, pupils.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`ABG on known FiO2 and PEEP gives the PaO2/FiO2 ratio.`,`Without a gas machine, use **SpO2/FiO2**; 315 equals PaO2/FiO2 of 300.`,`Chest X-ray: patchy bilateral peripheral opacities with a normal heart size.`,`BNP under 100 pg/mL argues strongly against heart failure.`,`Send blood cultures, malaria, dengue, scrub typhus and leptospira tests.`,`Never delay oxygen, antibiotics or referral for tests.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Treat the cause first**: antibiotics within 1 hour, source control, antidotes.`,`Target SpO2 **88-95%**; do not chase normal oxygenation.`,`High-flow nasal oxygen is preferred over conventional oxygen.`,`High-flow is a bridge; ROX index under 4.88 at 12 hours predicts failure.`,`No reflex fluid bolus to a hypoxic patient who is not hypotensive.`,`Phone the intensivist early; every suspected ARDS goes to ICU.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Tidal volume **6 mL/kg predicted body weight**, from height, never actual weight.`,`Plateau pressure **30 cmH2O or less**; driving pressure under 15.`,`Higher PEEP for moderate to severe ARDS; no staircase recruitment manoeuvres.`,`Permissive hypercapnia: accept pH down to about 7.20.`,`**Prone at least 16 hours a day** when PaO2/FiO2 is under 150.`,`Conservative fluids once shock resolves; dexamethasone early in moderate-severe ARDS.`,`VV-ECMO for very severe, reversible ARDS at an experienced centre.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Lung function is usually near normal by 6-12 months.`,`**Post-intensive care syndrome**: weakness, cognitive problems, depression, PTSD.`,`Only about half return to work at 1 year.`,`Stop ICU-started drugs such as sedatives, antipsychotics and PPIs.`,`Screen with PHQ-9 and GAD-7, and screen the carer too.`,`Vaccinate against influenza, pneumococcus and COVID-19.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in ARDS`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin`,`2 g IV daily plus 500 mg IV daily`,`5-7 days`,`Within 1 h after cultures`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly`,`De-escalate at 48-72 h`,`Hospital-acquired sepsis; renal dosing`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg twice daily IV or NG`,`7 days`,`Scrub typhus, leptospirosis cover`],[`Dexamethasone`,`20 mg IV daily, then 10 mg daily`,`5 days each`,`Not after day 14; avoid in influenza`],[`Cisatracurium`,`15 mg IV, then 37.5 mg/h`,`48 h`,`Not routine; deep sedation needed`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV`,`To MAP 65`,`Limit fluids alongside`],[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg IV, repeated`,`Once off vasopressors`,`Keep perfusion preserved`],[`Enoxaparin`,`40 mg SC daily`,`Throughout ICU stay`,`Reduce if CrCl under 30`]]},{heading:`Berlin severity`,columns:[`Severity`,`PaO2/FiO2`,`SpO2/FiO2`,`What changes`],rows:[[`Mild`,`201-300`,`236-315`,`High-flow, CPAP or NIV with close watch`],[`Moderate`,`101-200`,`149-235`,`Intubate, protective ventilation, higher PEEP`],[`Severe`,`100 or less`,`148 or less`,`Prone, consider paralysis, ECMO centre`]]},{heading:`ARDS against cardiogenic oedema`,columns:[`Feature`,`ARDS`,`Cardiogenic oedema`],rows:[[`History`,`Sepsis, pneumonia, aspiration`,`Heart disease, orthopnoea, PND`],[`Examination`,`Normal JVP, no S3`,`Raised JVP, S3, pink frothy sputum`],[`Chest X-ray`,`Patchy peripheral, normal heart`,`Bat's wing, cardiomegaly, Kerley B`],[`BNP`,`Under 100 pg/mL`,`Over 500 pg/mL`],[`Diuretic response`,`Little gain`,`Rapid relief`]]}],remember:[`Berlin TIOO: timing, imaging, origin, oxygenation on PEEP 5 or more.`,`60 on 60% oxygen equals a ratio of 100: severe.`,`Sepsis commonest overall; pneumonia commonest direct cause.`,`6 mL/kg predicted body weight, plateau 30 or less.`,`Prone 16 hours a day when the ratio is under 150; PROSEVA halved mortality.`,`Paraquat is the one ARDS where oxygen is restricted.`,`Survivors' burden is muscle, mind and mood.`]},updated:[`SSC 2026: antibiotics within 1 h for probable sepsis or shock, within 3 h after rapid assessment when sepsis is only possible and the patient is stable`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - ARDS`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute diffuse inflammatory **permeability lung injury** -> non-cardiogenic oedema, shunt, low compliance.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Berlin 2012 (T-I-O-O)**: within 1 week, bilateral opacities, not fully cardiac/fluid, PaO2/FiO2 on **PEEP 5 or more**: **mild 201-300, moderate 101-200, severe 100 or less**. **2023 global definition**: adds HFNO 30 L/min or more, **SpO2/FiO2 315 or less** (SpO2 97% or below), lung ultrasound.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Direct**: pneumonia (commonest direct), aspiration, contusion, drowning, inhalation. **Indirect**: **sepsis (commonest overall, about 40%)**, trauma, TRALI, pancreatitis, burns. India: **falciparum malaria, dengue, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, OP, paraquat**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Insult -> macrophage cytokines -> neutrophil injury of **type I pneumocytes + endothelium** -> protein-rich exudate, hyaline membranes, surfactant loss -> **shunt (refractory hypoxaemia)** + 'baby lung' -> proliferative (7-21 d) -> fibrotic phase in a minority.`],[`Key history`,`Breathlessness over hours-days after an insult; fever, aspiration, transfusion (TRALI within 6 h), pesticide, monsoon fever; cardiac history favours cardiogenic oedema; immunosuppression.`],[`Key examination`,`RR over 30, accessory muscles, cannot complete sentences, SpO2 low on 15 L/min NRB; **normal JVP, no S3**; source hunt (eschar, conjunctival suffusion, fasciculations); drowsiness or rising PaCO2 = pre-terminal.`],[`Investigations`,`ABG on known FiO2/PEEP; **SpO2/FiO2 equivalents 315 / 235 / 148**; CXR (bilateral, normal heart); lung ultrasound; echo; **BNP under 100** argues against HF; cultures before antibiotics, malaria, dengue, scrub, lepto, lactate.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`All four Berlin components; PaO2 mmHg / FiO2 fraction (60 on 0.6 = 100 = severe); excluded: ALI term and wedge pressure 18 cut-off.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Treat the cause**: antibiotics within 1 h of probable sepsis (ceftriaxone 2 g + azithromycin 500 mg; pip-taz if hospital-acquired), doxycycline 100 mg BD, artesunate; O2 target **SpO2 88-95%**; HFNO preferred over standard O2; **ROX under 4.88 at 12 h** = failure.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Tidal volume 6 mL/kg predicted body weight** (men 50 + 0.91 x (cm - 152.4)), **plateau 30 or less**, **driving pressure under 15**, higher PEEP if moderate-severe, permissive hypercapnia (pH 7.20), no staircase recruitment manoeuvres.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Prone 16 h or more/day** if PaO2/FiO2 under 150 (PROSEVA 16% vs 33%); NMB (cisatracurium 48 h) if dyssynchrony; **dexamethasone 20 mg x 5 d then 10 mg x 5 d** early moderate-severe (not after day 14, not influenza); **VV-ECMO** for very severe reversible ARDS.`],[`Treatment - supportive`,`**Conservative fluids once shock resolves** (FACTT); head up 30-45; enteral feed 24-48 h; light sedation, daily SBT; Hb threshold 7 g/dL; glucose 140-180; enoxaparin 40 mg; PPI only if indicated.`],[`Special groups`,`**Paraquat**: restrict O2 (SpO2 88-92%). **Pregnancy**: left lateral tilt, fetal heart. **COVID-19**: dexamethasone 6 mg x 10 days. **OP poisoning**: atropine to clear chest.`],[`Complications`,`VILI (volu-, baro-, atelect-, biotrauma), pneumothorax, acute cor pulmonale, VAP, ICU-acquired weakness, delirium, fibrosis, **post-intensive care syndrome**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`SpO2 under 90% on 15 L/min, RR over 30 with normal or rising PaCO2, exhaustion, new drowsiness, shock, PaO2/FiO2 under 100, ROX under 4.88 - refer every suspected ARDS to ICU while setting up oxygen.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Lung function near normal by 6-12 months (low DLCO commonest); **PICS** - weakness, cognition, PHQ-9/GAD-7, PTSD, carer strain; stop ICU-started drugs; rehab; vaccinate; spirometry + DLCO at 3-6 months.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SSC 2026**: antibiotics within 1 h for probable sepsis/shock (within 3 h if only possible and stable); ESICM 2023 and ATS 2024 ventilation and steroid advice unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`6 mL/kg of predicted, never actual, weight; prone needs staff not equipment; nitric oxide and recruitment improve numbers, not survival; paraquat is the one ARDS where oxygen is restricted.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected ARDS at first contact`,caption:`Condensed exam version for the clinic. [ESICM 2023, SSC 2026]`,steps:[{label:`Hypoxic, tachypnoeic after an insult`,detail:`Sit up, call for help, assess exhaustion`,tone:`warn`},{label:`O2: NRB 15 L/min or HFNO`,detail:`Target SpO2 88-95%; record FiO2 and SpO2 with times`},{label:`Cultures, then antibiotics within 1 h`,detail:`If sepsis probable or shock; add doxycycline, artesunate as indicated`},{label:`Cardiogenic or ARDS?`,detail:`JVP, S3, BNP, ultrasound, response to furosemide 40 mg + nitrate`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No reflex fluid bolus`,detail:`If shocked: 250-500 mL boluses + early noradrenaline`},{label:`Phone ICU and transfer early`,detail:`O2 for twice the journey, trained escort, written trend`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`ARDS escalation ladder`,caption:`Add a rung when the one below fails. [ATS 2024, ESICM 2023]`,steps:[{label:`Treat cause + HFNO / CPAP`,detail:`Bridge, not destination; ROX under 4.88 at 12 h = intubate`},{label:`Lung-protective ventilation`,detail:`6 mL/kg PBW, plateau 30 or less, driving pressure under 15`},{label:`Higher PEEP (P/F 200 or less)`,detail:`No staircase recruitment manoeuvres`},{label:`Prone 16 h or more/day (P/F under 150)`,detail:`PROSEVA mortality 16% vs 33%`,tone:`good`},{label:`Neuromuscular blockade + early steroid`,detail:`Cisatracurium 48 h; dexamethasone 20 mg x 5 d then 10 mg x 5 d`},{label:`VV-ECMO at expert centre`,detail:`Very severe reversible ARDS (EOLIA)`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Berlin severity with SpO2/FiO2 equivalents`,caption:`Grade without a gas machine. [Berlin 2012, Matthay 2024]`,columns:[`Severity`,`PaO2/FiO2 (PEEP 5+)`,`SpO2/FiO2`,`Mortality`,`Action`],rows:[[`Mild`,`201-300`,`236-315`,`About 27-35%`,`HFNO/CPAP, close watch`],[`Moderate`,`101-200`,`149-235`,`About 32-40%`,`Intubate, higher PEEP, prone if under 150`],[`Severe`,`100 or less`,`148 or less`,`About 45%`,`Prone, paralysis, ECMO call`]]}]},"emergency-head-injury":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`TBI is altered brain function or brain pathology caused by an external force.`,`About 60% of Indian head injuries come from road crashes, mostly two-wheelers.`,`**Primary injury** happens at impact and cannot be reversed.`,`**Secondary injury** develops over hours and is the target of all early care.`,`Secondary causes: hypoxia, hypotension, hypercarbia, hypoglycaemia, fever, seizures, raised ICP.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Grade severity by post-resuscitation GCS: **mild 13-15, moderate 9-12, severe 3-8**.`,`Report GCS as E, V and M plus pupils.`,`Focal injuries: extradural, subdural and intracerebral haematoma, contusion.`,`Diffuse injuries: concussion, diffuse axonal injury, diffuse swelling.`,`Basal skull fracture: raccoon eyes, Battle sign, CSF leak, haemotympanum.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Monro-Kellie: brain, blood and CSF share a fixed skull.`,`2. A growing clot first displaces CSF and venous blood, then ICP rises steeply.`,`3. **CPP equals MAP minus ICP**; target CPP is 60-70 mmHg.`,`4. The injured brain loses autoregulation, so one hypotensive episode doubles mortality.`,`5. Uncal herniation compresses the third nerve, dilating the pupil on the same side.`,`6. Cushing triad signals brainstem compression before tonsillar herniation.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Note the mechanism: speed, helmet, fall over 1 m or 5 stairs.`,`Ask about loss of consciousness, amnesia and a **lucid interval**.`,`Ask about vomiting, worsening headache, seizure and fluid from nose or ear.`,`Ask about **anticoagulants**, antiplatelets and age 65 or over.`,`In infants, check the story fits; think non-accidental injury.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Protect the cervical spine during xABCDE.`,`Score the **best motor response**; it is the most useful single number.`,`A newly dilated, sluggish pupil is the earliest sign of herniation.`,`Hypotension in head injury means **bleeding elsewhere**, except in infants.`,`A fall of 2 GCS points or 1 motor point needs urgent reassessment.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Non-contrast CT head** is the test of choice; skull X-ray has no role.`,`CT within 1 hour for GCS under 13 initially or under 15 at 2 hours.`,`Also CT within 1 hour for seizure, focal deficit, basal fracture or vomiting more than once.`,`CT within 8 hours for anyone on anticoagulants, even if well.`,`Children: use **PECARN**; no predictors means CT is not needed.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Intubate if **GCS 8 or less** or airway reflexes are lost.`,`Keep SpO2 over 94% and PaCO2 35-45 mmHg.`,`Keep systolic BP **100-110 mmHg or more**; no permissive hypotension.`,`Use isotonic saline and blood; no hypotonic or dextrose fluids.`,`Head up 30 degrees, neck neutral, treat fever, keep glucose normal.`,`**Tranexamic acid 1 g** within 3 hours in mild to moderate TBI.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Steroids are contraindicated**; they increased mortality in CRASH.`,`Herniation signs: mannitol 20% 0.25-1 g/kg or 3% saline as a bridge to surgery.`,`Brief hyperventilation only for active herniation.`,`Severe TBI: seizure prophylaxis for 7 days with phenytoin or levetiracetam.`,`Reverse warfarin with 4-factor PCC plus vitamin K 5-10 mg IV.`,`Use paracetamol for headache; avoid sedatives that mask GCS.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Extradural: evacuate if over **30 mL**, whatever the GCS.`,`Acute subdural: operate if over 10 mm thick or midline shift over 5 mm.`,`Only a **third** of extradurals show the classic lucid interval.`,`Chronic subdural causes reversible confusion in the elderly weeks after a trivial fall.`,`Diffuse axonal injury causes coma out of proportion to a near-normal CT.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer for a surgical lesion, GCS 8 or less, deterioration or CSF leak.`,`Transfer with a trained escort and speak directly to the neurosurgeon.`,`Discharge mild injury only to a **responsible adult** for 24 hours.`,`Give written return advice: drowsiness, repeated vomiting, seizure, weakness.`,`Concussion: rest 24-48 hours, then graded return to learning before sport.`,`Brainstem death: four-member board, two tests at least 6 hours apart.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in head injury`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g IV over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h`,`Start within 3 h`,`Mild-moderate TBI; seizures at high dose`],[`Mannitol 20%`,`0.25-1 g/kg IV over 15-20 min`,`For herniation only`,`Diuresis, hypotension, AKI`],[`Hypertonic saline 3%`,`2-5 mL/kg (about 250 mL) IV`,`Over 10-20 min`,`Hypernatraemia; preferred if hypotensive`],[`Levetiracetam`,`500-1000 mg 12-hourly (status 60 mg/kg)`,`7 days prophylaxis`,`Somnolence, irritability`],[`Phenytoin`,`20 mg/kg IV, then 100 mg 8-hourly`,`7 days`,`Hypotension, arrhythmia; in saline`],[`4-factor PCC with vitamin K`,`PCC 25-50 IU/kg plus vitamin K 5-10 mg IV`,`Once`,`Thrombosis`],[`Paracetamol`,`1 g 6-hourly; child 15 mg/kg`,`As needed`,`Max 4 g/day`]]},{heading:`Extradural against subdural`,columns:[`Feature`,`Extradural`,`Acute subdural`,`Chronic subdural`],rows:[[`Source`,`Middle meningeal artery`,`Torn bridging veins`,`Bridging veins, atrophic brain`],[`CT shape`,`Biconvex, stops at sutures`,`Crescent, crosses sutures`,`Crescent, hypodense`],[`Typical patient`,`Young adult, temporal fracture`,`High-energy injury`,`Elderly, on anticoagulants`],[`Course`,`Lucid interval in one-third`,`Unconscious from impact`,`Weeks of confusion, falls`],[`Prognosis`,`Excellent if drained early`,`Poor`,`Good; recurrence 10-20%`]]},{heading:`CT head within 1 hour (NICE NG232, adults)`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Threshold`],rows:[[`GCS on first assessment`,`Under 13`],[`GCS at 2 hours`,`Under 15`],[`Skull fracture`,`Suspected open, depressed or basal`],[`Seizure`,`Any post-traumatic seizure`],[`Neurology`,`Any focal deficit`],[`Vomiting`,`More than one episode`]]}],remember:[`Only secondary injury is treatable: prevent hypoxia and hypotension.`,`EDH is biconvex and stops at sutures; SDH is a crescent that crosses them.`,`Dilated pupil same side as the clot; weakness opposite (beware Kernohan notch).`,`Steroids harm (CRASH); TXA within 3 hours helps (CRASH-3).`,`Cushing triad: rising BP, falling pulse, irregular breathing.`,`Mannitol and hyperventilation are bridges to theatre.`,`Chronic subdural is a reversible dementia.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Head injury`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Alteration in brain function, or other evidence of brain pathology, from an **external force**; primary injury fixed at impact, **secondary injury preventable**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Post-resuscitation GCS**: mild 13-15, moderate 9-12, severe 3-8; morphology - **focal** (EDH, SDH, ICH, contusion) vs **diffuse** (concussion, DAI, swelling); skull fractures linear, depressed, compound, basal.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Two-wheeler crashes without helmets (about 60%), falls, assault; **age 65 or over, anticoagulants/antiplatelets**, alcohol, bleeding disorders; NAI in infants.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Monro-Kellie: haematoma -> CSF/venous displacement -> ICP rises steeply -> **CPP = MAP - ICP** falls (target 60-70) -> uncal herniation (ipsilateral CN III, contralateral hemiparesis) -> Cushing triad -> tonsillar herniation; lost autoregulation - SBP under 90 or PaO2 under 60 doubles mortality.`],[`Key history`,`Mechanism (fall over 1 m/5 stairs, ejection, pedestrian), LOC, **amnesia over 30 min**, **lucid interval**, vomiting more than once, seizure, headache, anticoagulants, age, what caused the fall (syncope, hypoglycaemia).`],[`Key examination`,`**GCS E-V-M + pupils** (GCS-P), lateralising signs, scalp boggy swelling/step, **basal skull signs** (raccoon eyes, Battle, CSF leak, haemotympanum), C-spine, Cushing response; obs half-hourly till GCS 15.`],[`Investigations`,`**Non-contrast CT** (no skull X-ray). **NICE NG232 CT within 1 h**: GCS under 13 initially, under 15 at 2 h, open/depressed or basal fracture, seizure, focal deficit, vomiting more than once. **Within 8 h**: anticoagulated, or LOC/amnesia + age 65+, bleeding disorder, dangerous mechanism. PECARN in children.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`xABCDE with C-spine; intubate **GCS 8 or less**; **SpO2 over 94%, PaCO2 35-45**, **SBP 100+ (50-69 y) / 110+ (15-49, over 70)** - no permissive hypotension, no dextrose fluids; head up 30, neck neutral; normoglycaemia, normothermia.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**TXA 1 g over 10 min + 1 g over 8 h within 3 h** (GCS 9-15, CRASH-3); **steroids contraindicated** (CRASH); herniation: **mannitol 0.25-1 g/kg** or **3% saline 2-5 mL/kg**; seizure prophylaxis 7 days in severe TBI; warfarin - **PCC 25-50 IU/kg + vitamin K**; dabigatran - idarucizumab 5 g; paracetamol for pain.`],[`Treatment - surgical lesions`,`**EDH**: biconvex, does not cross sutures, evacuate if over 30 mL or GCS under 9 with anisocoria. **Acute SDH**: crescentic, crosses sutures, operate if over 10 mm or shift over 5 mm. **Chronic SDH**: burr holes; MMA embolisation cuts recurrence.`],[`Special groups`,`**Infants**: hypotension from scalp/intracranial bleed, NAI, paediatric verbal score, PECARN. **Elderly**: chronic SDH as reversible dementia, anticoagulants. **Athletes**: concussion - 24-48 h rest, graded return to learn before sport, SCAT6.`],[`Complications`,`Herniation, post-traumatic seizures, CSF leak/meningitis, DAI, hypopituitarism, post-concussion syndrome (10-30% beyond 4 weeks), chronic SDH, brainstem death.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`GCS 8 or less or fall of 2 points (or 1 motor), dilating pupil, Cushing triad, seizure, repeated vomiting, CSF leak, anticoagulated, penetrating/depressed fracture; refer neurosurgery and transfer without waiting for repeat imaging.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Discharge mild TBI only to a **responsible adult for 24 h** with written return advice; review at 1-2 weeks; falls assessment in elderly; driving advice; **brainstem death** under THOT Rules 2014 - 4-member board, 2 sets 6 h apart, apnoea test.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Helmets (MV Amendment Act 2019), seat belts, no drink-driving; **Cashless Treatment Scheme 2025** up to Rs 1.5 lakh for 7 days.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - NICE NG232, BTF 4th edition and CRASH-3 TXA remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Only secondary injury is treatable; only a third of EDH show a lucid interval; hypotension in head injury means bleeding elsewhere; mannitol and hyperventilation are bridges to theatre.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Adult head injury: who needs CT now`,caption:`Condensed NICE NG232 decision. [NICE NG232 2023]`,steps:[{label:`xABCDE with C-spine; GCS E-V-M, pupils`,detail:`Intubate if GCS 8 or less; SBP 110+, SpO2 over 94%`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Any 1-hour CT criterion?`,detail:`GCS under 13, under 15 at 2 h, open/basal fracture, seizure, focal deficit, 2+ vomits`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes: CT within 1 hour`,detail:`TXA within 3 h if GCS 9-15; neurosurgery if lesion`},{label:`On anticoagulant, or LOC/amnesia + risk factor?`,detail:`Age 65+, bleeding disorder, dangerous mechanism, amnesia over 30 min`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes: CT within 8 hours`,detail:`Reverse anticoagulation if bleed`},{label:`No criteria: discharge with responsible adult`,detail:`Written return advice for 24-48 h`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Extradural against subdural haematoma`,caption:`The CT shape and the patient tell them apart. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,columns:[`Feature`,`Extradural`,`Acute subdural`,`Chronic subdural`],rows:[[`Source`,`Middle meningeal artery`,`Bridging veins + contusion`,`Bridging veins over atrophic brain`],[`CT shape`,`Biconvex, stops at sutures`,`Crescentic, crosses sutures`,`Crescentic, hypodense`],[`Course`,`Lucid interval in about one-third`,`Unconscious from impact`,`Weeks of confusion, falls`],[`Surgery`,`Over 30 mL or GCS under 9 + anisocoria`,`Over 10 mm or shift over 5 mm`,`Burr holes; MMA embolisation`],[`Prognosis`,`Excellent if early`,`Poor`,`Good; recurrence 10-20%`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Raised ICP: bridge to theatre`,caption:`Each rung buys minutes, not a cure. [BTF 2016]`,steps:[{label:`Head up 30, neck neutral, loosen ties`,detail:`Normothermia, normoglycaemia, treat seizures`},{label:`Oxygenate and normoventilate`,detail:`SpO2 over 94%, PaCO2 35-45 mmHg`},{label:`Protect SBP`,detail:`100-110 mmHg or more; isotonic fluids, no dextrose`},{label:`Osmotherapy`,detail:`Mannitol 20% 0.25-1 g/kg or 3% saline 2-5 mL/kg`},{label:`Brief hyperventilation`,detail:`PaCO2 30-35 only for active herniation`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Craniotomy / decompression`,tone:`good`}]}]},"emergency-snake-bite-envenomation":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Envenoming is the illness caused when a venomous snake injects venom.`,`**Dry bites** (20-50%) inject no venom and need no ASV.`,`ASV is given for **envenoming, never for the bite alone**.`,`India has about **58,000 snakebite deaths** a year, peaking in the monsoon.`,`NAPSE 2024 aims to halve deaths by 2030; snakebite is notifiable since November 2024.`,`Helpline **15400** guides victims to ASV-stocked facilities.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify by **syndrome, not by snake**; the syndrome guides treatment.`,`**Neurotoxic**: spectacled cobra and common krait.`,`**Haemotoxic**: Russell's viper and saw-scaled viper.`,`Russell's viper also causes AKI (nephrotoxic).`,`Polyvalent ASV covers the big four, not hump-nosed viper, king cobra or sea snakes.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Venom spreads through **lymphatics**, so immobilising the limb slows it.`,`2. Cobra toxin blocks acetylcholine receptors reversibly, so neostigmine can help.`,`3. Krait toxin destroys the nerve terminal, so neostigmine rarely helps.`,`4. Paralysis starts with ptosis, then bulbar palsy, neck weakness, then breathing.`,`5. Viper enzymes consume clotting factors, making blood **incoagulable**.`,`6. After ASV, the liver restores clotting factors over about 6 hours.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Note the **time of bite**; it sets the clock for tests and ASV.`,`Night bite while sleeping on the floor suggests **krait**.`,`Krait bites are painless and cause early abdominal pain.`,`Ask about heavy eyelids, double vision, nasal voice and difficulty swallowing.`,`Ask about bleeding gums, red urine and reduced urine output.`,`Never delay treatment to identify or catch the snake.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Absent fang marks do not exclude envenoming.`,`Mark the edge of swelling and measure limb girth hourly.`,`Ask the patient to look up to detect early **ptosis**.`,`Inability to lift the head (**broken neck sign**) precedes respiratory failure.`,`Single breath count under 10-15 is worrying.`,`A paralysed patient with fixed pupils may be **fully conscious**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Do the **20-minute whole blood clotting test** in a new, clean, dry glass tube.`,`Blood that runs out at 20 minutes is incoagulable and needs ASV.`,`If normal, repeat hourly for 3 hours, then 4-6 hourly to 24 hours.`,`Observe every bite for at least **24 hours**.`,`Check creatinine, potassium and hourly urine output in viper bites.`,`Never delay ASV for laboratory tests.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`First aid is **Do it RIGHT**: Reassure, Immobilise, Go to Hospital, Tell the doctor.`,`**No tourniquet**, cutting, sucking, ice or herbs.`,`Release an existing tourniquet only once ASV is running and adrenaline is ready.`,`ASV: **8-10 vials IV over 1 hour**; children get the same dose.`,`No skin test; keep **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM** drawn up.`,`Use paracetamol for pain; no NSAIDs or IM injections.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Haemotoxic: repeat 20WBCT **6 hours** after each dose; give 5-10 more vials until it clots.`,`Neurotoxic: give a second 10 vials at **1-2 hours** if no improvement.`,`Beyond **20 vials** in neurotoxic bites there is no benefit; ventilate.`,`Neostigmine trial: atropine 0.6 mg then neostigmine 1.5 mg IV.`,`Stop neostigmine if no response within 1 hour, as in krait bites.`,`ASV reaction: stop, adrenaline IM, then restart ASV slowly.`,`FFP only after ASV and only for serious active bleeding.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Russell's viper causes AKI; refer for dialysis if oliguric or potassium over 6.5.`,`Never perform fasciotomy while blood is incoagulable.`,`Serum sickness appears at 5-24 days after ASV.`,`Late hypopituitarism may follow Russell's viper bite with shock.`,`Scorpion sting can cause myocarditis and pulmonary oedema.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Give the **first ASV dose at the PHC** before referral.`,`Refer for ventilation, dialysis, persistent bleeding or no response after 20-30 vials.`,`Send vials given, times and 20WBCT results with the patient.`,`Scorpion sting: **prazosin**, not atropine; keep supine after the first dose.`,`Prevention: sleep on a cot under a tucked-in net, carry a torch at night.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in snake bite and scorpion sting`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Polyvalent ASV (haemotoxic)`,`8-10 vials IV over 1 h`,`5-10 vials 6-hourly until clotting`,`Anaphylaxis 10-20%; no skin test`],[`Polyvalent ASV (neurotoxic)`,`10 vials IV over 1 h`,`Second 10 at 1-2 h; max 20`,`Ventilate rather than exceed 20`],[`Adrenaline 1:1000`,`0.5 mg IM; child 0.01 mg/kg`,`Repeat in 5-10 min`,`For ASV anaphylaxis`],[`Atropine then neostigmine`,`0.6 mg IV, then 1.5 mg IV`,`If response, 0.5 mg every 30 min x 5`,`Bradycardia, secretions; stop if no response`],[`Prednisolone`,`5 mg 6-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`For serum sickness`],[`Prazosin (scorpion)`,`500 microgram oral; child 30 microgram/kg`,`Repeat at 3 h, then 6-hourly`,`First-dose hypotension`],[`Paracetamol`,`15 mg/kg 6-hourly`,`As needed`,`Only safe analgesic here`]]},{heading:`The big four snakes`,columns:[`Snake`,`Syndrome`,`Local signs`,`Key feature`],rows:[[`Spectacled cobra`,`Neurotoxic (post-synaptic)`,`Pain, swelling, necrosis`,`Responds to neostigmine`],[`Common krait`,`Neurotoxic (pre-synaptic)`,`Minimal; night bite`,`Delayed paralysis, long ventilation`],[`Russell's viper`,`Haemotoxic, nephrotoxic`,`Marked swelling`,`Bleeding, shock, AKI`],[`Saw-scaled viper`,`Haemotoxic`,`Swelling, local bleeding`,`Prolonged coagulopathy`]]},{heading:`Scorpion sting grades`,columns:[`Grade`,`Features`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`1`,`Local pain only`,`Lignocaine, paracetamol, observe 6 h`],[`2`,`Sweating, vomiting, priapism, tachycardia`,`Prazosin; antivenom if available`],[`3`,`Myocarditis, pulmonary oedema`,`Prazosin, nitroglycerin, dobutamine, ICU`],[`4`,`Multi-organ failure`,`ICU with ventilation`]]}],remember:[`Do it RIGHT: Reassure, Immobilise, Go to Hospital, Tell the doctor.`,`ASV for envenoming, not for the bite.`,`Same ASV dose for children: the snake injects the same venom.`,`20WBCT in a new, clean, dry glass tube; repeat 6 hours after each dose.`,`Neurotoxic: 10 vials, second 10 at 1-2 hours, then ventilate.`,`Neostigmine for cobra, rarely for krait.`,`Prazosin, not atropine, for scorpion sting.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Snake bite and scorpion sting`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Envenoming = clinical syndrome after venom injection; **dry bite in 20-50%** - **ASV for envenoming, never for the bite alone**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By **syndrome**: neurotoxic (cobra, krait), haemotoxic (Russell's, saw-scaled), nephrotoxic (Russell's), myotoxic (sea snakes), local cytotoxic. **Big four** covered by polyvalent ASV; not covered: hump-nosed pit viper, king cobra, sea snakes.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`India about **58,000 deaths/year**; monsoon, rural farm workers, sleeping on the floor (krait), paddy fields (vipers); delay at traditional healers.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Cobra post-synaptic alpha-toxin (neostigmine-responsive) vs krait **presynaptic beta-bungarotoxin** (not); paralysis order: ptosis -> bulbar -> neck -> respiratory. Viper procoagulants -> **VICC** -> incoagulable blood (20WBCT); AKI, capillary leak, pituitary haemorrhage.`],[`Key history`,`**Time of bite**, circumstances, snake description (never delay), neuro symptoms (heavy lids, diplopia, dysphagia), bleeding, dark urine, **painless night bite + abdominal pain (krait)**, first aid used, tetanus status, pregnancy.`],[`Key examination`,`ABC; bite site, tender nodes, **mark swelling and measure hourly**; ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, **broken neck sign**, single breath count; bleeding sites; compartment signs; paralysed patients are conscious - **never brain dead**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Envenoming (= ASV indication)**: incoagulable 20WBCT or coagulopathy, spontaneous bleeding, neurotoxic signs (ptosis), shock/arrhythmia, AKI, haemo/myoglobinuria; **local**: swelling over **half the limb within 48 h**, rapid extension, digit bites, tender nodes. No signs = dry bite - observe 24 h, no ASV.`],[`Investigations`,`**20WBCT**: 2 mL in new clean dry glass tube, 20 min, tilt - runs = incoagulable; normal -> hourly x 3 then 4-6 hourly to 24 h; after ASV **6 h after each dose**; observe **24 h**; PT/INR, creatinine, K+, CK, urine, ECG, hourly urine output.`],[`Treatment - first aid`,`**Do it RIGHT**: Reassure, Immobilise, Go to Hospital (108), Tell doctor; remove rings; **no tourniquet, cut, suck, ice, herbs**; pressure bandage not routine; release existing tourniquet only with ASV running and adrenaline ready.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Polyvalent ASV 8-10 vials IV over 1 h** (in 5-10 mL/kg saline, or push 2 mL/min); **same dose in children**; **no skin test**, no routine premedication; **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM drawn up**; give the **first dose before referral**; never prophylactic for a dry bite.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Haemotoxic**: 20WBCT at 6 h, if incoagulable 5-10 vials 6-hourly until clotting (usually under 25-30). **Neurotoxic**: no better at **1-2 h** -> second 10 vials; **max 20**, then ventilate.`],[`Treatment - adjuncts`,`**Neostigmine trial**: atropine 0.6 mg then neostigmine 1.5 mg IV (child atropine 0.05 + neostigmine 0.04 mg/kg); response in 1 h -> 0.5 mg every 30 min x 5. BVM/intubation for paralysis; FFP only after ASV for serious bleeding; no heparin/TXA; dialysis for AKI.`],[`Complications - ASV reactions`,`Early (10-20%): stop, **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM**, hydrocortisone, chlorpheniramine, **restart slowly**; pyrogenic: paracetamol, fluids; serum sickness 5-24 days: prednisolone 5 mg 6-hourly 5-7 days.`],[`Special groups - scorpion sting`,`Indian red scorpion opens Na channels -> autonomic storm -> catecholamine myocarditis, pulmonary oedema; **prazosin 500 microgram (child 30 microgram/kg) 3-hourly then 6-hourly**; lignocaine for local pain; antivenom + prazosin; **no atropine or steroids**; NTG/dobutamine for oedema.`],[`Special groups`,`**Children**: same ASV dose. **Pregnancy**: same dose, risk of abortion/APH. **Anticoagulated**: higher bleeding risk. Hump-nosed viper: ASV ineffective - supportive.`],[`Complications`,`Respiratory paralysis, intracranial bleed, **AKI**, shock, compartment syndrome (rare - never fasciotomy before clotting corrects), necrosis, **late hypopituitarism** after Russell's viper, CKD.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Ptosis or broken neck sign, incoagulable blood, oliguria/dark urine, shock, rapidly extending swelling, anaphylaxis to ASV, no response after 20 (neuro) or 25-30 (haemo) vials; **give first ASV dose before referral**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NAPSE 2024** - halve deaths by 2030, ASV at every PHC/CHC, helpline **15400**; snakebite **notifiable on IHIP (Nov 2024)**; cot and tucked net, torch, boots; PHC stock 10-20 vials + new glass tubes.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - MoHFW STG 2017 dosing remains the national protocol; NAPSE 2024 and notifiability (Nov 2024) already included.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Children get the same 8-10 vials; no skin test; 20WBCT needs a clean glass tube; neostigmine works for cobra, rarely krait; prazosin, not atropine, for scorpion sting.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Snakebite at the PHC`,caption:`Condensed national protocol. [MoHFW STG Snakebite 2017]`,steps:[{label:`Arrival: ABC, IV line in unbitten limb`,detail:`20WBCT, labs, mark swelling; tourniquet off only when ready`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Signs of envenoming?`,detail:`Incoagulable 20WBCT, bleeding, ptosis, shock, AKI, swelling over half limb`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No: observe 24 h`,detail:`20WBCT hourly x 3, then 4-6 hourly; neuro checks`},{label:`Yes: ASV 8-10 vials IV over 1 h`,detail:`Same dose in children; no skin test; adrenaline drawn up`},{label:`Neurotoxic: neostigmine trial, BVM ready`,detail:`No better at 1-2 h -> 10 more vials; max 20, then ventilate`},{label:`Haemotoxic: 20WBCT 6 h after each dose`,detail:`Incoagulable -> 5-10 vials; repeat until clots`},{label:`Refer with vials and times recorded`,detail:`Ventilation, dialysis, persistent bleeding`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`The big four at a glance`,caption:`Syndrome guides ASV and support. [WHO SEARO 2016]`,columns:[`Snake`,`Syndrome`,`Local`,`Key feature`,`Neostigmine`],rows:[[`Spectacled cobra`,`Neurotoxic (post-synaptic)`,`Pain, swelling, necrosis`,`Ptosis to respiratory paralysis`,`Responds`],[`Common krait`,`Neurotoxic (pre-synaptic)`,`None; night floor bite`,`Abdominal pain, delayed paralysis`,`Poor`],[`Russell's viper`,`Haemotoxic, nephrotoxic`,`Marked swelling`,`Incoagulable blood, AKI, hypopituitarism`,`Not used`],[`Saw-scaled viper`,`Haemotoxic`,`Swelling, local bleed`,`Prolonged coagulopathy`,`Not used`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Scorpion sting grades`,caption:`Grade drives care. [Bawaskar JAPI 2012]`,steps:[{label:`Grade 1: local pain only`,detail:`Lignocaine infiltration, paracetamol, observe 6 h`},{label:`Grade 2: autonomic storm`,detail:`Prazosin 500 microgram (child 30 microgram/kg) 3-hourly then 6-hourly; antivenom`},{label:`Grade 3: myocarditis, pulmonary oedema`,detail:`Prazosin, O2, NTG 5-20 microgram/min, dobutamine; ICU`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Grade 4: multi-organ failure`,detail:`ICU with ventilation`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"emergency-heat-illness":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Heat illness occurs when heat gain exceeds heat loss and core temperature rises.`,`**Heat stroke** is core temperature above **40 C with CNS dysfunction**.`,`Sweating does **not** exclude heat stroke.`,`**Heat exhaustion** is core 37-40 C with a **normal mental state**.`,`India's heat season runs March to July.`,`IMD heat wave in plains: maximum at least 40 C with departure 4.5-6.4 C, or 45 C actual.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Spectrum: heat rash, oedema, cramps, syncope, exhaustion and heat stroke.`,`Mental state, not sweating, separates exhaustion from heat stroke.`,`**Classic heat stroke**: elderly and chronically ill, over days of a heat wave.`,`**Exertional heat stroke**: fit young people, over hours of work or sport.`,`Exertional heat stroke brings more rhabdomyolysis, AKI and DIC.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Humidity stops sweat evaporating, so humid heat is more lethal.`,`Unacclimatised new workers are most at risk; acclimatisation takes 7-14 days.`,`Vulnerable: over 65, infants, pregnancy, heart failure, CKD, dementia, living alone.`,`Anticholinergics block sweating; diuretics and SGLT2 inhibitors deplete volume.`,`Beta-blockers limit cardiac output; lithium toxicity rises with dehydration.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The hypothalamus drives skin vasodilatation and sweating to lose heat.`,`2. When cardiac output fails, gut vessels constrict and endotoxin leaks out.`,`3. Cytokines produce a **sepsis-like inflammatory response**.`,`4. Above about 42 C, heat directly denatures proteins and damages cells.`,`5. Injury depends on **temperature multiplied by duration**, so cooling speed decides survival.`,`6. Cerebellar Purkinje cells are most sensitive, so **ataxia** appears early.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about outdoor work, exertion, shade and water, especially 12 noon to 3 pm.`,`Any behaviour change in a hot patient is heat stroke until proved otherwise.`,`Ask about plain water without salt, vomiting and dark cola urine.`,`Review drugs: anticholinergics, diuretics, SGLT2 inhibitors, ACE inhibitors, lithium.`,`Ask about fever before exposure, rigors or rash; malaria and meningitis mimic heat stroke.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Measure **rectal temperature**; oral, axillary and tympanic readings mislead.`,`Check GCS and cerebellar signs: ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus.`,`Skin is hot and dry in classic, often sweating in exertional heat stroke.`,`Focal deficits or neck stiffness point away from heat stroke.`,`Look for muscle tenderness, petechiae and jaundice.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Check rectal temperature every 5 minutes during cooling.`,`Check capillary glucose and ECG monitoring at the bedside.`,`Send electrolytes, creatinine, LFT, **CK**, platelets and INR; repeat at 24-72 hours.`,`Do a malaria test in every febrile confused patient.`,`**Never delay cooling** for any test.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Cool first, transport second**; never send a hot patient uncooled.`,`Aim for **below 39 C within 30 minutes** of collapse.`,`**Cold water immersion** is the gold standard: ice water up to the neck.`,`Remove from water at **38.5-39 C** to avoid overshoot.`,`If immersion is impossible, use tarp-assisted or evaporative cooling with fanning.`,`Ice packs and cold IV saline are adjuncts only.`,`Heat exhaustion: cool place, ORS 200-400 mL at a time, observe.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Antipyretics are contraindicated**; the hypothalamic set point is normal.`,`Dantrolene has no role; it is for malignant hyperthermia.`,`Benzodiazepines stop shivering and seizures during cooling.`,`Give cautious crystalloid 1-2 L in the first hour; elderly develop pulmonary oedema easily.`,`Rhabdomyolysis: saline to urine output 200-300 mL/hour.`,`Treat hyperkalaemia with calcium gluconate, insulin-glucose and salbutamol.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer every heat stroke once cooling has started; keep cooling en route.`,`Monitor 24-72 hours for delayed liver injury, DIC and AKI.`,`Report cases on **IHIP** from 1 March to 31 July under NPCCHH.`,`Ahmedabad 2013 was South Asia's first heat action plan.`,`Prevention: water often, avoid outdoor work 12-3 pm, never leave children in parked cars.`,`Review diuretics, SGLT2 inhibitors and ACE inhibitors before summer.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in heat illness`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Midazolam or lorazepam`,`Midazolam 2-5 mg IV; lorazepam 2-4 mg IV`,`Repeat once`,`Respiratory depression, hypotension`],[`Saline or Ringer lactate`,`1-2 L IV in first hour; child 10-20 mL/kg`,`Guided by urine and lungs`,`Pulmonary oedema in elderly`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV`,`After fluids and cooling`,`Arrhythmia, extravasation`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`10 mL IV over 10 min`,`Repeat if ECG changes persist`,`For hyperkalaemia`],[`Dextrose 25%`,`100 mL IV; child 10% 2-5 mL/kg`,`Once, recheck`,`Check glucose in all confused`],[`ORS`,`200-400 mL sips at a time`,`Through recovery`,`Not for the confused patient`],[`Paracetamol, NSAIDs`,`Do not use`,`Not applicable`,`No effect; worsen liver or kidney`]]},{heading:`Heat illness by severity`,columns:[`Condition`,`Core temperature`,`Key features`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Heat cramps`,`Normal`,`Spasm after plain water replacement`,`Rest, ORS`],[`Heat syncope`,`Normal or mildly raised`,`Faint on standing`,`Lie flat, legs up, fluids`],[`Heat exhaustion`,`37-40 C`,`Weak, vomiting; mental state normal`,`Cool place, ORS, observe`],[`Heat stroke`,`Above 40 C`,`Confusion, ataxia, seizures, coma`,`Immediate immersion, refer`]]},{heading:`Classic against exertional heat stroke`,columns:[`Feature`,`Classic`,`Exertional`],rows:[[`Who`,`Elderly, infants, chronically ill`,`Young fit workers and athletes`],[`Onset`,`Days of a heat wave`,`Hours of exertion`],[`Sweating`,`Often absent`,`Often present`],[`Rhabdomyolysis, AKI, DIC`,`Less common`,`Common`],[`Mortality`,`Above 50% if cooling delayed`,`Near zero with immediate cooling`]]}],remember:[`Heat stroke: above 40 C plus brain dysfunction; sweating does not exclude it.`,`Cool first, transport second: below 39 C within 30 minutes.`,`Immerse in ice water; stop at 38.5-39 C.`,`No antipyretics: the set point is normal.`,`Rectal thermometer only; others mislead.`,`Dantrolene is for malignant hyperthermia, not heat stroke.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Heat-related illness`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Heat gain exceeds loss and core temperature rises. **Heat stroke = core over 40 C + CNS dysfunction** (sweating does not exclude); heat exhaustion = 37-40 C with **normal mental state**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Spectrum: heat rash, oedema, cramps, syncope, **exhaustion, stroke**; heat stroke **classic** (elderly, days, dry) vs **exertional** (young, hours, sweating, rhabdo/AKI/DIC). IMD heat wave: 40 C plains / 37 coast / 30 hills with departure 4.5-6.4, or 45 C actual.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Heat stroke** = **rectal temperature over 40 C + CNS dysfunction** (confusion, ataxia, seizure, coma) after heat exposure or exertion - sweating does not exclude; **heat exhaustion** = 37-40 C with **normal mental state**. Exclude malaria, meningitis, NMS, serotonin syndrome, thyroid storm, MH.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Heat waves March-July, humidity (WBGT), outdoor labour 12-3 pm, no acclimatisation (7-14 days), age over 65, infants, pregnancy, CKD, HF, obesity; **anticholinergics, diuretics, SGLT2i, ACEi/ARB, beta-blockers, lithium**, stimulants, alcohol.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Hypothalamic set point normal -> heat loss overwhelmed -> splanchnic vasoconstriction -> gut endotoxin -> **SIRS-like cytokine storm** + direct cytotoxicity over 42 C -> cerebellar Purkinje injury, rhabdomyolysis, AKI, liver injury, **DIC at 24-72 h**.`],[`Key history`,`Exposure and exertion, collapse during work vs days at home, **any behaviour change**, fluids (plain water - cramps), cola urine, drugs, vulnerability, infection clues (malaria, meningitis), toxidrome (NMS, serotonin, MH, thyroid storm).`],[`Key examination`,`**Rectal temperature mandatory** (oral/tympanic underestimate); GCS, **ataxia**, seizures; tachycardia, hypotension; hot dry or sweating skin; muscle tenderness; crackles; jaundice; rigidity (NMS), clonus (serotonin).`],[`Investigations`,`Rectal temp every 5 min during cooling, glucose, ECG; electrolytes, Ca, phosphate, creatinine, LFT, **CK (over 5x = rhabdo)**, PT/INR, platelets (repeat 24-72 h); gas; **malaria test in every febrile confused patient**; CT/LP only after cooling starts.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Cool first, transport second**: **cold water immersion (1-15 C)** to **below 39 C within 30 min**, stop at **38.5-39 C**; else tarp-assisted or evaporative (spray + fan); ice packs adjunct only; ABC, O2, glucose.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**No antipyretics** (set point normal; NSAIDs worsen AKI); **no dantrolene**; benzodiazepine for shivering/seizures (midazolam 2-5 mg IV); NS/RL **1-2 L in first hour** (cautious in elderly); noradrenaline if still hypotensive; rhabdo - urine 200-300 mL/h.`],[`Treatment - minor heat illness`,`Rash - cotton, calamine; cramps - ORS or salted fluids; syncope - lie flat, fluids; oedema - elevate, **no diuretics**; exhaustion - cool place, **ORS 200-400 mL at a time**, IV if vomiting; any confusion = heat stroke.`],[`Special groups`,`**Elderly**: classic heat stroke, pulmonary oedema with fluids, drug review. **Children**: parked vehicles, IV 10-20 mL/kg. **Workers**: acclimatisation, work-rest, buddy system. **Pregnancy**: higher risk, cool aggressively.`],[`Complications`,`Rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, AKI, DIC, acute liver failure, ARDS, cerebral oedema, rebound hyperthermia or overshoot hypothermia, lasting cerebellar deficits (20-30%).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any confusion, ataxia or seizure in heat; rectal over 40 C; exertional collapse; cola urine; bleeding/jaundice at 24-72 h; refractory hypotension; refer every heat stroke **after cooling has begun**, cooling en route.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Repeat LFT, INR, platelets, creatinine at 24-72 h; return to exertion after 1-2 weeks rest with graded re-acclimatisation; pre-summer medicine review (diuretics, SGLT2i, ACEi sick-day rules, lithium).`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NPCCHH (NCDC)**: daily IHIP heat surveillance **1 March-31 July**, facility preparedness (cool room, ice, ORS corners, rectal thermometers), emergency cooling guidance; **NDMA heat action plans** (Ahmedabad 2013 first); IMD colour warnings 5 days ahead.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - NCDC summer 2025 advisory reaffirms the 2024 NPCCHH action plan, emergency cooling guidance and IHIP reporting from 1 March; ACSM 2023 cooling targets unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Rectal temperature or nothing; cool first, transport second; antipyretics do not work; dantrolene is for malignant hyperthermia; cerebellum is most heat-sensitive.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected heat stroke: cool first`,caption:`Condensed exam version. [ACSM Consensus 2023, NPCCHH Heat Guidelines 2024]`,steps:[{label:`Hot patient with any mental change`,detail:`Rectal temperature; do not wait for it after exertional collapse`,tone:`warn`},{label:`ABC, O2, capillary glucose`,detail:`Recovery position; intubate if GCS stays 8 or less`},{label:`Immersion possible?`,detail:`Cold water tub, tarp or ice water`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes: cold water immersion 1-15 C`,detail:`Rectal temp every 5 min; below 39 C within 30 min`},{label:`No: evaporative or tarp cooling`,detail:`Strip, spray lukewarm water, fan; ice packs to neck, axillae, groin`},{label:`Stop at 38.5-39 C`,detail:`Watch rebound and overshoot; benzodiazepine for shivering`},{label:`Fluids, bloods, malaria test, transfer`,detail:`1-2 L in first hour; CK, LFT, INR; keep cooling en route`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Heat exhaustion against heat stroke`,caption:`Mental state, not sweating, separates them. [Harrison 22e]`,columns:[`Feature`,`Heat exhaustion`,`Heat stroke`],rows:[[`Core temperature`,`37-40 C`,`Above 40 C`],[`Mental state`,`Normal`,`Confusion, ataxia, seizures, coma`],[`Sweating`,`Heavy`,`Absent (classic) or present (exertional)`],[`Organ injury`,`None`,`Rhabdo, AKI, liver, DIC`],[`Treatment`,`Cool place, ORS or IV fluids, observe`,`Immediate immersion, ABC, refer`]]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Classic against exertional heat stroke`,caption:`Two patients, one emergency. [ACSM Consensus 2023]`,columns:[`Feature`,`Classic`,`Exertional`],rows:[[`Who`,`Elderly, infants, chronically ill`,`Young workers, athletes, soldiers`],[`Onset`,`Days of heat wave`,`Hours of exertion`],[`Skin`,`Often dry`,`Often sweating`],[`Rhabdo, AKI, DIC`,`Less common`,`Common`],[`Mortality`,`Over 50% if cooling delayed`,`Near zero with immediate immersion`]]}]}};export{e as default};